-- Home-system outpost overview screen, shown after a Guest picks a
-- faction (main/faction_select.gui_script). Three tabs:
--
-- **Overview** (default, per direct instruction) — a read-only summary of
-- the player's status/progress (faction, home system, active ship +
-- fitting completeness, fleet size, owned-component/upgrade counts) built
-- entirely from real session/ships/catalog data, not an invented
-- "progress %" metric (plan.md §0/§4's "don't invent unconfirmed numbers"
-- rule extends to not inventing progression systems that don't exist
-- either). Navigating to the other tabs is via the shared tab bar only -
-- no separate "Go to" links on the page itself, removed as redundant.
--
-- **Fitting** — left side: a top-down visual of the player's
-- active ship with one marker per fitting slot, placed at that slot's own
-- {x, y} screen-pixel offset from the ship visual's center
-- (main/data/ships.lua's `slot_positions` — RANDOM PLACEHOLDER
-- coordinates for now, see plan.md §4). Right side: two always-visible
-- lists, **Shop** (purchasable, not yet owned) and **Owned** (spares not
-- installed). Drag a card from either list onto a matching slot marker to
-- install it; drag an installed marker off to uninstall it.
--
-- **Ships** — a tab for selecting a different owned ship or purchasing a
-- new one, per direct instruction (the other local reference project has
-- its own version of this, ~/Defold/SuperShips/main/station.gui_script's
-- ship-picker: buttons grouped by class, colored locked/unlocked/
-- selected, clicking one calls its own set_ship_type — same overall idea
-- of "click a ship entry to make it the active one," built fresh here
-- rather than ported, since our roster/economy differ enough that
-- reusing that script directly wouldn't fit). Two always-visible lists:
-- **Ships For Sale** (not yet owned) and **Owned Ships** (click to
-- select). Only one ship exists in the roster so far (Patrol 1, §2.1.2),
-- so For Sale is empty and Owned has exactly one, correctly so.
--
-- **Skins** — every skin for the ACTIVE ship's species (main/data/skins.lua)
-- plus its default hull, as thumbnail cards (main/images/skins/, generated by
-- tools/build_skin_index.py). Equipped/owned skins are framed green/blue;
-- clicking an owned one equips it straight away, clicking one you don't own
-- opens a purchase modal. Equip state lives in main/session.lua. In flight,
-- main/skin_flight.lua shows recolors and surface themes; sculpted themes and
-- Clockwork show the default model until Live Update.
--
-- A Launch button (visible on both tabs) enters flight mode — not built
-- yet, just logs the intent.
--
-- This is the docked equivalent of the reference project's in-flight
-- component panel (~/Defold/SuperShips/main/hud.gui_script's
-- components_panel: one colored box per slot, green when filled,
-- positioned over a background via a stored per-slot local coordinate) —
-- same idea, but interactive/drag-and-drop instead of a read-only
-- flight-HUD status display, and the slot positions are plotted directly
-- on a ship silhouette rather than a separate abstract icon panel.
--
-- Components can only be fitted here, at an outpost (§2.8) — there's no
-- flight-mode screen yet to do this from mid-flight anyway.
--
-- A Scrip balance readout sits outside all three tabs (always visible,
-- per direct instruction) and updates after every purchase/sale -
-- module and ship prices/refunds are flat PLACEHOLDER values from
-- main/session.lua (not real per-item economy design yet, §2.6/§4), but
-- the balance itself and its live updates are real.
--
-- Ship/loadout/owned data comes from main/session.lua; display names come
-- from main/data/modules/catalog.lua and main/data/ships.lua; the
-- outpost's own system is the one the player docked in (docked_system_id
-- below: their current system if their faction has an outpost there,
-- otherwise home).

local session = require("main.session")
local star_systems = require("main.data.star_systems")
local ships = require("main.data.ships")
local catalog = require("main.data.modules.catalog")
local skins = require("main.data.skins")
local skin_collections = require("main.data.skin_collections")
local skin_flight = require("main.skin_flight")
local network = require("main.network")
local profile = require("main.profile")
local ranks = require("main.data.ranks")

local FACTION_DISPLAY = { accord = "The Accord", swarm = "The Swarm" }

-- Falls back to this if a ship has no `slot_positions` of its own yet -
-- a slot with no stored position is simply not shown, same as the
-- reference project's own "no position, no box" rule.
local SLOT_TYPE_BY_PREFIX = { W = "weapon", C = "computer", E = "engine", H = "hull" }

-- Generic icon shown for an EMPTY slot of each type (main/images/icons/
-- Octagon *.png, hand-provided art, added to main/images/icons.atlas
-- alongside the procedurally-generated icons - per direct instruction,
-- replacing the old "slot_<type>_empty" outline icons generated by
-- tools/build_module_icons.py).
local SLOT_EMPTY_ICON = { weapon = "Octagon W", computer = "Octagon C", engine = "Octagon E", hull = "Octagon H" }

-- Square, not the old 110x60 landscape rectangle - a REGULAR octagon
-- (equal side lengths) needs a square bounding box; new_octagon_node's
-- 8-vertex pie is inscribed in an ellipse that only degenerates into a
-- circle (and therefore a regular octagon) when width == height.
local MARKER_SIZE = vmath.vector3(74, 74, 0)
-- Colour of the in-flight key under each slot marker ("Shift+1", "Key 3").
local HOTKEY_LABEL_COLOR = vmath.vector4(1.0, 0.85, 0.35, 1.0)
-- Slightly transparent per direct instruction (alpha 0.65, was fully
-- opaque) - lets the ship visual underneath show through the slot
-- markers a bit rather than fully hiding it. Scoped to just the marker's
-- own background box; the icon/labels drawn on top of it, and the
-- hover-highlight border/invalid-mark, stay fully opaque so they're
-- still clearly legible.
local SLOT_FILLED_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.3, 0.8, 0.45, 0.65)
local SLOT_EMPTY_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.35, 0.38, 0.42, 0.65)
-- Advanced-only slot, not yet unlocked (direct instruction: grey out and
-- disable rather than hide - see build_markers' own `locked` comment).
-- Dimmer and flatter than SLOT_EMPTY_COLOR so it reads as unavailable
-- rather than just another empty slot.
local SLOT_LOCKED_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.18, 0.19, 0.2, 0.45)
local SLOT_LOCKED_ICON_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5)

-- Weapon firing-arc hover indicator (plan.md §2.8's `angle_deg`/`arc`
-- provision) - a translucent wedge shown while hovering an OCCUPIED weapon
-- slot on the Fitting screen, centered on that slot's own mount point.
-- Radius kept modest (120px) since ship_visual (main/outpost.gui) is only
-- 260px wide (+-130 from center) - a much bigger wedge would spill past the
-- panel into the Shop/Owned lists beside it.
local WEAPON_ARC_SIZE = vmath.vector3(240, 240, 0)
local WEAPON_ARC_COLOR = vmath.vector4(1.0, 0.85, 0.25, 0.35)
local WEAPON_ARC_PERIMETER_VERTICES = 48 -- smooth circle, not a faceted polygon like the octagon markers

-- Confirm dialog Yes/No button layout (positions authored in
-- main/outpost.gui: confirm_yes at x=820, confirm_no at x=1100,
-- confirm_panel centered at x=960 - symmetric +-140 either side).
-- Reused to re-center Yes when show_confirm's show_cancel is false.
local CONFIRM_YES_X = 820
local CONFIRM_YES_CENTERED_X = 960
local CONFIRM_BUTTON_Y = 460

-- Launch countdown (per direct instruction: "when in an outpost and launch
-- is triggered, countdown five seconds before launching") - see the
-- launch_node handler in on_input and the countdown tick in update() below.
local LAUNCH_COUNTDOWN_SEC = 5

-- Sector Map tab (new) - all 58 main/data/star_systems.lua systems plotted
-- as dot+label pairs, ported from the reference project's own
-- main/map.gui_script + main/sector_map.lua (per direct instruction) -
-- same "create the 58 nodes once, just show/hide/recolor on open" pattern,
-- since the map plots static data that never changes mid-session (there's
-- no flight mode yet, §2.10/§4, so a system's `map_x`/`map_y` position and
-- which one counts as "current" - see refresh_map below - never move
-- during a session either).
--
-- Unlike the reference (which plotted map_x/map_y as a DIRECT 1:1 screen
-- offset - its own comment noted the source data happened to already be a
-- screen-appropriate scale for its smaller map overlay), `MAP_SCALE` here
-- is computed at build_map_dots() time from the real data bounds (found
-- via a one-off luajit scan of star_systems.lua: x spans [-385.5, 372.5],
-- y spans [-230.5, 180.5], so a naive 1:1 plot would only fill a small
-- fraction of `map_panel`'s much bigger 1720x620 area) rather than
-- hardcoded, so it stays correct if a system's position (or the roster
-- itself) ever changes.
local MAP_PANEL_CENTER = vmath.vector3(960, 460, 0) -- matches main/outpost.gui's "map_panel" node position
local MAP_PANEL_HALF_SIZE = vmath.vector3(1720 / 2, 620 / 2, 0) -- matches "map_panel"'s size
local MAP_MARGIN = 70 -- panel-edge padding so dots/labels never clip the panel border
local MAP_DOT_SIZE = vmath.vector3(14, 14, 0)
local MAP_DOT_SIZE_CURRENT = vmath.vector3(22, 22, 0) -- grown, not just recolored - same reasoning as MARKER_SIZE's own "color alone isn't enough" precedent elsewhere in this file
local MAP_LABEL_OFFSET_Y = 16
local MAP_LABEL_SCALE = vmath.vector3(0.38, 0.38, 1)
local MAP_DOT_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.5, 0.55, 0.62, 1.0) -- neutral grey - every system except the current one
local MAP_CURRENT_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.4, 0.9, 1.0, 1.0) -- cyan, same color the reference used for "where you are right now"

-- Icon shown per slot (main/images/icons.atlas) — a filled module's own
-- icon (catalog.lua's `icon` field) when installed, otherwise a generic
-- octagon icon for that slot's TYPE (SLOT_EMPTY_ICON above, "Octagon W"
-- etc.), so an empty slot still shows what kind of module belongs there.
-- This replaces showing the installed item's name as text (plan.md §4).
local ICON_TEXTURE = "icons"
-- Per direct instruction: the new Octagon-icon set (§2.8.1) needs to be
-- sized up to match the grey/colored octagon marker box (`box`,
-- MARKER_SIZE) it sits on, rather than the old, much smaller 48x48 icon
-- footprint — the Octagon art is itself a complete octagon graphic (its
-- own border/fill drawn in the image), meant to cover the marker's whole
-- visible face, not float as a small badge in the middle of it.
local ICON_SIZE = MARKER_SIZE
local CARD_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.16, 0.2, 0.26, 1.0)
-- Per direct instruction: Shop and Owned (spares) cards are color-coded by the
-- module's slot TYPE, matching the same weapon=gold/engine=red/computer=blue/
-- hull=green hue family as the Octagon slot-marker icons (§2.8.1) - so a
-- card's color previews which slot it fits before you even drag it. Falls
-- back to the plain CARD_COLOR above for a module whose type isn't one of
-- the four (shouldn't happen, but don't guess a color for it either).
local TYPE_CARD_COLOR = {
	weapon = vmath.vector4(0.42, 0.34, 0.12, 1.0),
	engine = vmath.vector4(0.42, 0.16, 0.16, 1.0),
	computer = vmath.vector4(0.16, 0.22, 0.42, 1.0),
	hull = vmath.vector4(0.16, 0.36, 0.22, 1.0),
}
-- Brighter version of each TYPE_CARD_COLOR hue, used for the Fitting tab's
-- four type-filter buttons (§2.8.8) when that type is the active filter -
-- same dim TYPE_CARD_COLOR when not selected, so the buttons preview the
-- same color-coding the cards themselves use.
local FILTER_ACTIVE_COLOR = {
	weapon = vmath.vector4(0.85, 0.68, 0.22, 1.0),
	engine = vmath.vector4(0.85, 0.25, 0.25, 1.0),
	computer = vmath.vector4(0.25, 0.35, 0.85, 1.0),
	hull = vmath.vector4(0.25, 0.7, 0.35, 1.0),
}
-- Fixed display order for the four filter buttons - matches the existing
-- SLOT_TYPE_ORDER convention used elsewhere on this screen (Overview tab's
-- fitting breakdown).
local FILTER_TYPE_ORDER = { "weapon", "computer", "engine", "hull" }
local CARD_SIZE = vmath.vector3(820, 50, 0)
local CARD_SPACING = 58
-- Shop/Owned module list rows (direct instruction: show each module's icon
-- next to its name) - a small square icon at the card's own left edge,
-- same ICON_TEXTURE atlas the fitting slot markers already use, with the
-- label left-aligned right after it rather than centered across the whole
-- card.
local ROW_ICON_SIZE = vmath.vector3(36, 36, 0)
local ROW_ICON_MARGIN = 14
local ROW_ICON_GAP = 10
-- Small "Sell" button pinned to the right edge of a sellable card (Owned
-- spares, Owned Ships - never Shop/For Sale, §2.8/§4's "there needs to be
-- an option to sell a ship or sell a component" instruction). Its own
-- node, separate from the card's own box/label, so it doesn't collide
-- with that card's existing click behavior (dragging a module card,
-- clicking a ship card to select it).
local SELL_BUTTON_SIZE = vmath.vector3(90, 36, 0)
-- Ship detail modal's bottom button row (direct instruction: up to 4 can
-- apply at once now - Select/Purchase, Advance, Sell, Cancel) - laid out
-- dynamically each time show_ship_detail runs (evenly spaced, centered on
-- the panel) rather than four fixed authored positions, so 1-4 visible
-- buttons never leaves an awkward gap where a hidden one would've been.
local DETAIL_BUTTON_W = 220
local DETAIL_BUTTON_H = 80
local DETAIL_BUTTON_GAP = 20
local DETAIL_BUTTON_Y = 260
local DETAIL_BUTTON_CENTER_X = 960
local SELL_BUTTON_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.5, 0.2, 0.22, 1.0)
-- Shared by the Fitting tab's two card-list panels (shop/owned) - both are
-- authored at this same height in main/outpost.gui. Used to
-- clip each list to its own panel and to figure out how far it's allowed
-- to scroll (plan.md §4 - the catalog outgrew the old unscrollable,
-- always-show-everything panel, e.g. once the mining naming-scale
-- additions took the Shop list to 6 items).
local PANEL_HEIGHT = 220
local SCROLL_STEP = CARD_SPACING -- one row per wheel notch

-- Hover feedback while dragging (drag-over highlight): a "border" box
-- sized a bit bigger than the marker itself, created BEHIND it in the
-- node list (added first, so the marker's own filled box sits on top and
-- only the border's overflow edge shows through) - the cheap flat-color
-- way to fake a glow/border effect without a real outline/nine-patch
-- texture. Neon cyan when the dragged item's type matches this slot,
-- neon red (plus a big red "X" text on top of everything) when it
-- doesn't.
local BORDER_SIZE = vmath.vector3(MARKER_SIZE.x + 20, MARKER_SIZE.y + 20, 0)
-- Drag ghost (direct instruction: show the dragged module's icon, not its
-- name) - a small square backdrop plus the icon on top, same layered
-- pattern as a fitting slot marker's own box+icon.
local GHOST_SIZE = vmath.vector3(64, 64, 0)
local GHOST_ICON_SIZE = GHOST_SIZE
local BORDER_VALID_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.3, 1.0, 0.9, 1.0)
local BORDER_INVALID_COLOR = vmath.vector4(1.0, 0.15, 0.2, 1.0)
local INVALID_MARK_COLOR = vmath.vector4(1.0, 0.1, 0.1, 1.0)

local TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE = vmath.vector4(0.22, 0.38, 0.5, 1.0)
local TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE = vmath.vector4(0.15, 0.17, 0.22, 1.0)
local SHIP_CARD_CURRENT_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.2, 0.45, 0.3, 1.0) -- the ship you're currently flying/fitting
-- Owned but not currently active (direct instruction: the merged Ships grid
-- highlights any owned hull, not just the active one, since it's now the
-- only place to see - and swap to - the rest of your fleet). A cooler blue
-- than SHIP_CARD_CURRENT_COLOR's green so "owned" and "owned AND active"
-- stay visually distinct at a glance.
local SHIP_CARD_OWNED_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.2, 0.32, 0.48, 1.0)

-- Ships grid (direct instruction: grid layout with a live 3D preview per
-- cell, replacing the old single-column text list - just for this one
-- panel). Column count/cell size are derived from forsale_panel's own
-- known size (main/outpost.gui) rather than a fresh independent guess -
-- same footing as CARD_SIZE/CARD_SPACING above being sized against that
-- panel's own 820px width.
--
-- One row per ship CLASS, one column per ROLE (direct instruction): rather
-- than packing for-sale ships sequentially into a column wrap, each ship's
-- cell position is looked up directly from its own ship_id (every id in
-- main/data/ships.lua is exactly "<class>_<role>", e.g. "frigate_tactical")
-- via CLASS_ROW/ROLE_COL below - so a ship always sits in the same cell
-- regardless of purchase order. 3 classes x 4 roles = GRID_COLS/
-- CLASS_ORDER's own length.
local CLASS_ORDER = { "patrol", "escort", "frigate" }
local ROLE_ORDER = { "interceptor", "support", "assault", "tactical" }
local CLASS_ROW, ROLE_COL = {}, {}
for i, key in ipairs(CLASS_ORDER) do CLASS_ROW[key] = i end
for i, key in ipairs(ROLE_ORDER) do ROLE_COL[key] = i end

local GRID_COLS = #ROLE_ORDER
-- All 3 class rows visible at once, no scrolling (direct instruction: with
-- the separate Owned Ships section gone, this one panel now owns the whole
-- middle of the screen - forsale_panel grew from 220 to 620px tall,
-- main/outpost.gui, specifically so every row fits without a scroll step).
-- SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT is deliberately its own constant, not the shared
-- PANEL_HEIGHT every other card-list panel (shop/owned/fitting) still uses
-- at the original 220px.
local GRID_ROWS_VISIBLE = #CLASS_ORDER
-- Shrunk from 620 to make room for the column-header strip just above the
-- panel (direct instruction: label each column/row with its role/class).
local SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT = 575
local GRID_GUTTER = 10
local GRID_CELL_W = (1200 - (GRID_COLS + 1) * GRID_GUTTER) / GRID_COLS
local GRID_CELL_H = (SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT - (GRID_ROWS_VISIBLE + 1) * GRID_GUTTER) / GRID_ROWS_VISIBLE
local GRID_LABEL_H = 26 -- bottom strip of each Skins-grid card reserved for its name
-- Ships grid: a taller strip with the ship's name on the top line and its
-- price (or Current/Owned) on a second, smaller line.
local SHIP_LABEL_H = 50
local SHIP_NAME_SCALE = vmath.vector3(0.8, 0.8, 1)
local SHIP_DETAIL_SCALE = vmath.vector3(0.6, 0.6, 1)
local SHIP_NAME_OFFSET_Y = 10 -- name above the strip's centre, detail below
local SHIP_DETAIL_OFFSET_Y = -12
-- Half a row per wheel notch (not a full row) - finer-grained on top of the
-- two-row change above, and further smoothed by the animated scroll below
-- (SCROLL_LERP_SPEED) so a notch no longer feels like an abrupt snap.
local SCROLL_STEP_GRID = (GRID_CELL_H + GRID_GUTTER) / 2
-- Animated scroll (direct instruction): this file's own `update(self, dt)`
-- lerps self.scroll_display.forsale toward the target self.scroll.forsale
-- by this fraction of the remaining distance per second (exponential
-- smoothing) - see apply_forsale_scroll_display's own comment below.
local SCROLL_LERP_SPEED = 12
-- Drag-to-scroll (direct instruction): a Ships-For-Sale press only resolves
-- as a click (opens the ship detail modal) if the cursor stayed within this
-- many pixels of where it was pressed - otherwise it's treated as a
-- drag-scroll instead, so dragging never accidentally opens a ship's detail
-- modal mid-scroll. See on_input's own handling of self.forsale_press.
local DRAG_CLICK_THRESHOLD = 6

-- Skins tab grid: 13 cards (12 collections + Default) in a 5x3 grid, in a
-- panel authored at forsale_panel's exact size (main/outpost.gui's
-- skins_panel). Each card is a colored frame (status) around a thumbnail,
-- with a name strip along the bottom and a status/price badge top-left.
local SKIN_GRID_COLS = 5
local SKIN_GRID_ROWS = 3
local SKIN_CELL_W = (1200 - (SKIN_GRID_COLS + 1) * GRID_GUTTER) / SKIN_GRID_COLS
local SKIN_CELL_H = (SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT - (SKIN_GRID_ROWS + 1) * GRID_GUTTER) / SKIN_GRID_ROWS
local SKIN_FRAME = 4 -- frame thickness left showing around the thumbnail
local SKIN_THUMB_ASPECT = 336 / 240 -- tools/build_skin_index.py's THUMB_SIZE
local SKIN_THUMB_BG = vmath.vector4(0.2, 0.245, 0.29, 1.0) -- matches the renders' own backdrop
local SKIN_BADGE_SIZE = vmath.vector3(150, 28, 0)
local SKIN_EQUIPPED_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.35, 0.85, 0.45, 1.0)
local SKIN_OWNED_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.3, 0.55, 0.9, 1.0)
local SKIN_PRICE_BADGE_COLOR = vmath.vector4(0.42, 0.34, 0.12, 0.95)
local SKIN_BADGE_SHADE = vmath.vector4(0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 1.0) -- badges use a darker frame color so white text reads on them
-- Thumbnails of skins you don't own yet are dimmed so owned ones stand out.
local SKIN_LOCKED_TINT = vmath.vector4(0.6, 0.6, 0.65, 1.0)
local SKIN_KIND_LABEL = { recolor = "Recolor", surface = "Surface theme", model = "Sculpted model" }

-- Forward-declared: needs PREVIEW_WORLD_POS/PREVIEW_CAMERA (defined much
-- further down, next to show_ship_detail) but is called from
-- refresh_ship_cards (defined well before those tables) - see its actual
-- definition below (next to compute_forsale_preview_items) for what it does.
local refresh_forsale_preview

function init(self)
	self.active = false
	self.active_page = "overview" -- "overview" | "fitting" | "ships" - defaults to Overview per direct instruction
	self.dragging = nil -- { source = "slot"/"owned"/"shop", item = key, from_slot = id or nil, type = module type }
	self.markers = {} -- slot_id -> { box, label, border, invalid_mark, type }
	self.hovered_slot = nil -- slot_id currently highlighted while dragging, or nil
	self.cards = {} -- Fitting tab's draggable module cards: { box, label, item_key, source }
	self.ship_cards = {} -- Ships tab's clickable ship cards: { box, label, ship_id, source }
	self.skin_cards = {} -- Skins tab's cards: { box, skin_id (nil = default hull), owned, equipped }
	self.skin_textures = {} -- thumbnail name -> gui texture id, loaded while the Skins tab is open
	-- Skin purchase modal: nil when closed, else { skin_id, thumb = node }.
	-- Reuses the ship detail modal's nodes (show_skin_detail).
	self.skin_detail = nil
	self.ghost = nil -- { box, label } following the cursor while dragging
	-- Fitting tab's type filter (§2.8.8, per direct instruction): nil shows
	-- every type in Shop/Owned as before; set to "weapon"/"computer"/
	-- "engine"/"hull" narrows both lists to just that type. Toggled by
	-- clicking a filter button - clicking the already-active one clears it
	-- back to nil (show everything) rather than needing a separate "All"
	-- button. Deliberately NOT reset on tab switch/re-entry - it's a
	-- standing preference for the session, same as the scroll offsets below.
	self.type_filter = nil
	-- Per-list scroll offset in pixels, one entry per card-list panel -
	-- 0 = scrolled to the top. Adjusted by mouse wheel (on_input) and
	-- clamped against each list's own current row count every time it's
	-- rebuilt (refresh_module_cards/refresh_ship_cards), since the row
	-- count - and therefore how far a list is even allowed to scroll -
	-- can change between rebuilds (an item gets bought, a ship gets
	-- added to the roster, etc.).
	self.scroll = { shop = 0, owned = 0, forsale = 0 }
	-- Currently-DISPLAYED scroll position for the Ships-For-Sale grid, kept
	-- separate from self.scroll.forsale (the TARGET) - update()'s own
	-- per-frame lerp eases this toward the target on wheel-scroll (direct
	-- instruction: smooth animated scrolling instead of an instant snap).
	self.scroll_display = { forsale = 0 }
	-- Set on a Ships-For-Sale grid press, resolved on release as either a
	-- click (opens that card's ship detail modal) or a drag-scroll,
	-- depending on how far the cursor moved in between (direct instruction:
	-- drag-to-scroll, not just mouse wheel) - see on_input's own handling.
	self.forsale_press = nil

	msg.post(".", "acquire_input_focus")
	msg.post("#gui", "disable")

	self.title_node = gui.get_node("title")
	self.ship_node = gui.get_node("ship_overview")
	self.launch_node = gui.get_node("launch_button")
	self.launch_countdown_node = gui.get_node("launch_countdown")
	gui.set_enabled(self.launch_countdown_node, false)
	self.launching = false -- true during the LAUNCH_COUNTDOWN_SEC delay between clicking Launch and actually starting flight - see on_input's launch_node handler and update() below
	self.launch_countdown_time_left = 0
	-- Currency/resource balance readouts (plan.md §4/§2.6, per direct
	-- instruction: "always show what currency the player has" - later
	-- expanded to "show all currencies ... script, water, iron, and
	-- whatever was the fourth resource" i.e. Hydrogen, §2.6's fuel
	-- resource) - all four sit outside every tab's static-node group, so
	-- none are ever hidden regardless of which tab is active.
	-- refresh_currency (below) keeps all four in sync.
	self.scrip_node = gui.get_node("scrip_label")
	self.water_node = gui.get_node("water_label")
	self.iron_node = gui.get_node("iron_label")
	self.hydrogen_node = gui.get_node("hydrogen_label")
	-- Account (top-left, outside every tab): saves, then opens the website's
	-- account pages in place of the game (logging out and everything else
	-- account-related happens there, not in the game).
	self.logout_node = gui.get_node("logout_button")

	self.tab_nodes = {
		overview = gui.get_node("tab_overview"),
		fitting = gui.get_node("tab_fitting"),
		ships = gui.get_node("tab_ships"),
		map = gui.get_node("tab_map"),
		skins = gui.get_node("tab_skins"),
	}

	-- Overview tab's static nodes. No "Go to Fitting"/"Go to Ships" links
	-- on the page itself (removed per direct instruction - the shared tab
	-- bar at the top already covers that, a second way to do the same
	-- thing was redundant).
	self.overview_content_node = gui.get_node("overview_content")
	self.overview_static_nodes = {
		gui.get_node("overview_heading"), gui.get_node("overview_panel"),
		self.overview_content_node,
	}

	-- Fitting tab's static nodes (enabled/disabled together on tab switch)
	self.ship_visual_node = gui.get_node("ship_visual")
	-- Weapon firing-arc hover wedge (plan.md §2.8) - one reusable TYPE_PIE
	-- node, repositioned/reshaped and toggled per hovered slot rather than
	-- built once per marker like build_markers' own per-slot nodes, since
	-- only one can ever be shown at a time (whatever's under the cursor).
	-- Same TYPE_PIE setup as new_octagon_node (further down this file) but
	-- WEAPON_ARC_PERIMETER_VERTICES-sided (a smooth circle, not an 8-sided
	-- octagon) and a variable fill/rotation instead of a fixed 360.
	self.weapon_arc_node = gui.new_pie_node(gui.get_position(self.ship_visual_node), WEAPON_ARC_SIZE)
	gui.set_perimeter_vertices(self.weapon_arc_node, WEAPON_ARC_PERIMETER_VERTICES)
	gui.set_outer_bounds(self.weapon_arc_node, gui.PIEBOUNDS_ELLIPSE)
	gui.set_inner_radius(self.weapon_arc_node, 0)
	gui.set_pivot(self.weapon_arc_node, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
	gui.set_color(self.weapon_arc_node, WEAPON_ARC_COLOR)
	gui.set_enabled(self.weapon_arc_node, false)
	self.weapon_arc_hover_slot = nil -- slot_id the wedge is currently shown for, or nil
	self.shop_panel_node = gui.get_node("shop_panel")
	self.owned_panel_node = gui.get_node("owned_panel")
	-- Type-filter buttons (§2.8.8) - one box per type, keyed the same as
	-- TYPE_CARD_COLOR/FILTER_ACTIVE_COLOR/FILTER_TYPE_ORDER above.
	self.filter_nodes = {
		weapon = gui.get_node("filter_weapon"),
		computer = gui.get_node("filter_computer"),
		engine = gui.get_node("filter_engine"),
		hull = gui.get_node("filter_hull"),
	}
	-- Ship Statistics panel (per direct instruction: the Fitting tab's
	-- left-hand side - previously just the ship visual, centered in an
	-- 880px-wide zone with a lot of unused margin either side of the
	-- 260px-wide visual itself - splits into two 420px-wide halves, visual
	-- on the left (main/outpost.gui repositioned its x from 480 to 260,
	-- the left half's own center) and this new stats readout on the
	-- right). ship_stats_content_node's text is set by refresh_ship_stats
	-- further down this file - called on every Fitting-tab (re)entry and
	-- loadout change (from refresh_markers), and with a live highlight
	-- while a drag hovers a vacant matching slot (from update_hover).
	self.ship_stats_content_node = gui.get_node("ship_stats_content")
	self.fitting_static_nodes = {
		gui.get_node("ship_visual_heading"), self.ship_visual_node,
		gui.get_node("ship_stats_heading"), gui.get_node("ship_stats_panel"), self.ship_stats_content_node,
		gui.get_node("shop_heading"), self.shop_panel_node,
		gui.get_node("owned_heading"), self.owned_panel_node,
		self.filter_nodes.weapon, gui.get_node("filter_weapon_label"),
		self.filter_nodes.computer, gui.get_node("filter_computer_label"),
		self.filter_nodes.engine, gui.get_node("filter_engine_label"),
		self.filter_nodes.hull, gui.get_node("filter_hull_label"),
	}

	-- Ships tab's static nodes. Single merged panel (direct instruction: no
	-- more separate "Owned Ships" section - owned ships now just appear
	-- highlighted, in place, inside this same grid; see ship_forsale_rows).
	self.forsale_panel_node = gui.get_node("forsale_panel")
	-- Ships-For-Sale grid's scroll affordances (direct instruction) - static
	-- chrome authored in main/outpost.gui, shown/hidden and repositioned by
	-- refresh_forsale_scrollbar rather than rebuilt per-refresh like the
	-- cards themselves. Sized to fit all 3 class rows at once now (see
	-- GRID_ROWS_VISIBLE below), so these stay hidden in practice - kept
	-- rather than removed in case a future ship added mid-row-matrix ever
	-- needs scrolling again.
	self.forsale_scrollbar_track = gui.get_node("forsale_scrollbar_track")
	self.forsale_scrollbar_thumb = gui.get_node("forsale_scrollbar_thumb")
	self.forsale_scroll_up = gui.get_node("forsale_scroll_up")
	self.forsale_scroll_down = gui.get_node("forsale_scroll_down")
	self.ships_static_nodes = {
		gui.get_node("forsale_heading"), self.forsale_panel_node,
		self.forsale_scrollbar_track, self.forsale_scrollbar_thumb,
		self.forsale_scroll_up, self.forsale_scroll_down,
		-- Column (role) / row (class) headers (direct instruction) - purely
		-- static labels, positions hand-computed to match GRID_CELL_W/H's own
		-- formula (main/outpost.gui) since the panel's size never changes at
		-- runtime; no per-refresh rebuild needed the way the cards themselves get.
		gui.get_node("col_header_interceptor"), gui.get_node("col_header_support"),
		gui.get_node("col_header_assault"), gui.get_node("col_header_tactical"),
		gui.get_node("row_header_patrol"), gui.get_node("row_header_escort"),
		gui.get_node("row_header_frigate"),
	}

	-- Skins tab's static nodes. Cards are rebuilt on every visit/change by
	-- refresh_skin_cards, parented under skins_panel for its stencil clipping.
	self.skins_heading_node = gui.get_node("skins_heading")
	self.skins_panel_node = gui.get_node("skins_panel")
	self.skins_static_nodes = {
		self.skins_heading_node, gui.get_node("skins_hint"), self.skins_panel_node,
	}

	-- Map tab's static nodes, plus its 58 per-system dot+label pairs -
	-- created once here (ported approach, per direct instruction, from the
	-- reference project's own main/map.gui_script), not rebuilt per visit
	-- like Fitting's markers/Ships' cards, since the map plots
	-- main/data/star_systems.lua's static roster: nothing about a system's
	-- own position ever changes mid-session. set_page's "map" branch just
	-- toggles these on/off and calls refresh_map (further down this file)
	-- to recolor/resize whichever one is "current".
	self.map_static_nodes = {
		gui.get_node("map_heading"), gui.get_node("map_panel"),
		gui.get_node("map_current_text"),
	}
	self.map_current_text_node = gui.get_node("map_current_text")
	do
		-- Scale-to-fit (plan.md's own "derive from real geometry, don't
		-- hardcode a guess" convention, e.g. slot_positions' px/meter note) -
		-- computed from the actual bounds of every system's map_x/map_y
		-- rather than a hand-picked constant, so this stays correct if the
		-- roster or any position in it ever changes.
		local min_x, max_x, min_y, max_y = math.huge, -math.huge, math.huge, -math.huge
		for _, sys in pairs(star_systems.SYSTEMS) do
			min_x = math.min(min_x, sys.map_x)
			max_x = math.max(max_x, sys.map_x)
			min_y = math.min(min_y, sys.map_y)
			max_y = math.max(max_y, sys.map_y)
		end
		local data_center = vmath.vector3((min_x + max_x) / 2, (min_y + max_y) / 2, 0)
		local data_half = vmath.vector3(math.max(max_x - min_x, 1) / 2, math.max(max_y - min_y, 1) / 2, 0)
		local panel_half_usable = MAP_PANEL_HALF_SIZE - vmath.vector3(MAP_MARGIN, MAP_MARGIN, 0)
		local map_scale = math.min(panel_half_usable.x / data_half.x, panel_half_usable.y / data_half.y)

		self.map_dots = {} -- system_id -> { box = node, label = node }
		for id, sys in pairs(star_systems.SYSTEMS) do
			local pos = MAP_PANEL_CENTER + vmath.vector3(
				(sys.map_x - data_center.x) * map_scale,
				(sys.map_y - data_center.y) * map_scale,
				0)

			local box = gui.new_box_node(pos, MAP_DOT_SIZE)
			gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_color(box, MAP_DOT_COLOR)
			gui.set_enabled(box, false)

			local label = gui.new_text_node(pos + vmath.vector3(0, MAP_LABEL_OFFSET_Y, 0), sys.name)
			gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
			gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_scale(label, MAP_LABEL_SCALE)
			gui.set_color(label, MAP_DOT_COLOR)
			gui.set_enabled(label, false)

			self.map_dots[id] = { box = box, label = label }
		end
	end

	-- Each of the three card-list panels clips its own cards (stencil,
	-- so cards scrolled past the panel's edge are actually hidden, not
	-- just positioned off the visible list) - set once here since these
	-- are the same static authored panel nodes for the screen's whole
	-- lifetime, unlike the cards themselves which get rebuilt constantly.
	for _, panel_node in ipairs({
		self.shop_panel_node, self.owned_panel_node,
		self.forsale_panel_node, self.skins_panel_node,
	}) do
		gui.set_clipping_mode(panel_node, gui.CLIPPING_MODE_STENCIL)
		gui.set_clipping_visible(panel_node, true)
	end

	-- Confirmation dialog (plan.md §4/§2.8 - "any kind of purchase or
	-- swap needs a confirmation dialogue", per direct instruction).
	-- Authored hidden in main/outpost.gui; shown/hidden as a group via
	-- show_confirm/hide_confirm below. self.confirm is nil whenever the
	-- dialog is closed, or { on_confirm = function() ... end } while
	-- it's open and waiting on a Confirm/Cancel click (on_input's very
	-- first check, once self.active, gates everything else on this).
	self.confirm = nil
	self.confirm_nodes = {
		scrim = gui.get_node("confirm_scrim"),
		panel = gui.get_node("confirm_panel"),
		message = gui.get_node("confirm_message"),
		yes = gui.get_node("confirm_yes"),
		yes_label = gui.get_node("confirm_yes_label"),
		no = gui.get_node("confirm_no"),
		no_label = gui.get_node("confirm_no_label"),
	}
	for _, node in pairs(self.confirm_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, false)
	end

	-- Ship detail modal (§2.8.9, per direct instruction: clicking a ship card
	-- in the Ships tab shows this instead of going straight to the plain
	-- confirm dialog) - self.ship_detail is nil whenever closed, or
	-- { ship_id = ... } while open. Its own Purchase/Select button acts
	-- immediately (no follow-up confirm) - see show_ship_detail/on_input.
	self.ship_detail = nil
	self.ship_detail_nodes = {
		scrim = gui.get_node("ship_detail_scrim"),
		panel = gui.get_node("ship_detail_panel"),
		name = gui.get_node("ship_detail_name"),
		preview = gui.get_node("ship_detail_preview"),
		stats = gui.get_node("ship_detail_stats"),
		primary = gui.get_node("ship_detail_primary"),
		primary_label = gui.get_node("ship_detail_primary_label"),
		cancel = gui.get_node("ship_detail_cancel"),
		cancel_label = gui.get_node("ship_detail_cancel_label"),
		-- Sell (direct instruction: with the separate Owned Ships list gone,
		-- selling a ship - other than your last one, session.sell_ship's own
		-- guard - now happens from here instead of a card's own inline Sell
		-- button) - only shown when show_ship_detail decides it applies.
		sell = gui.get_node("ship_detail_sell"),
		sell_label = gui.get_node("ship_detail_sell_label"),
		-- Advance (direct instruction): upgrades an owned ship to its
		-- `advanced` component-slot tier (main/data/ships.lua) in place,
		-- one-way - only shown for an owned, not-yet-advanced ship whose
		-- data actually has an `advanced` table (not every ship gets one).
		advance = gui.get_node("ship_detail_advance"),
		advance_label = gui.get_node("ship_detail_advance_label"),
	}
	for _, node in pairs(self.ship_detail_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, false)
	end
end

local CURRENCY_NAME = { scrip = "Scrip", hydrogen = "Hydrogen" }

-- 36000 -> "36,000"
local function with_commas(n)
	local s = tostring(math.floor(n))
	local formatted = s:reverse():gsub("(%d%d%d)", "%1,"):reverse()
	return (formatted:gsub("^,", ""))
end

-- "36,000 Scrip", "600,000 Hydrogen", or "Not for sale" for a ship with no
-- price (the starter ship).
local function ship_price_text(ship_id)
	local amount, currency = session.get_ship_price(ship_id)
	if not amount then
		return "Not for sale"
	end
	return with_commas(amount) .. " " .. CURRENCY_NAME[currency]
end

local function ship_display_name(ship_id, faction)
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
	local skin = ship and ship.faction_skins[faction]
	return (skin and skin.name ~= "<TBD>") and skin.name or ship_id
end

-- The component slot counts actually in effect for `ship_id` right now:
-- `ship.advanced` once session.is_ship_advanced(ship_id) is true, otherwise
-- the normal `ship.components` - the single place this swap happens, so
-- every reader (Overview tab's fitting breakdown, the ship detail modal's
-- stats text) stays in sync automatically rather than each needing its own
-- is_ship_advanced check. Falls back to `ship.components` (or {}) for a
-- ship with no `advanced` table at all - not every ship has one yet (direct
-- instruction: only some ships get an advanced option).
local function effective_components(ship_id)
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
	if not ship then
		return {}
	end
	if session.is_ship_advanced(ship_id) and ship.advanced then
		return ship.advanced
	end
	return ship.components or {}
end

-- Real stat block, shared by the Ships tab's ship detail modal and the
-- Fitting tab's Ship Statistics panel (below) - straight from
-- main/data/ships.lua's `data` (Hull/Engine/FTL/Computer baseline,
-- §2.1.1) and `components` (W/C/E/H slot counts, §2.8), same numbers the
-- Overview tab's fitting breakdown already reads. A curated subset, not
-- every field in `data` - legible in a fixed-size panel rather than a
-- full engineering dump - covering every stat any current Hull/Engine
-- module can affect (main/data/modules/hull_modules.lua/engine_modules.lua's
-- own `stats` field) so `highlight` below always has a real line to flag.
--
-- `highlight` (optional): a set {stat_field = true, ...} of these same
-- `data` field names - per direct instruction ("when a component is
-- hovered over a vacant slot show the revised statistics"), flags
-- whichever stats a hovered drag would affect with a trailing "(+)"
-- marker. NOT a numeric delta - no Hull/Engine module has a real bonus
-- AMOUNT yet (plan.md §2.8.11's own "don't invent unconfirmed numbers"
-- finding - BSGO never published real numbers for these two module
-- types), so this can only show WHICH stats would change, not by how
-- much yet.
local function ship_stats_text(ship_id, highlight)
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
	if not ship then
		return ""
	end
	local d = ship.data or {}
	local c = effective_components(ship_id)
	local function mark(...)
		if not highlight then
			return ""
		end
		for _, field in ipairs({...}) do
			if highlight[field] then
				return " (+)"
			end
		end
		return ""
	end
	local lines = {
		"Class: " .. (ship.class or "?"),
		"",
		"Hull Points: " .. (d.hull_points or "?") .. mark("hull_points"),
		"Armor: " .. (d.armor or "?") .. mark("armor"),
		"Critical Defense: " .. (d.critical_defense or "?") .. mark("critical_defense"),
		"Speed: " .. (d.speed_m_per_sec or "?") .. " m/s  (Boost: " .. (d.boost_speed_m_per_sec or "?") .. " m/s)"
			.. mark("speed_m_per_sec", "boost_speed_m_per_sec"),
		"Turning Speed: " .. (d.turning_speed_deg_per_sec or "?") .. " deg/s" .. mark("turning_speed_deg_per_sec"),
		"FTL Range: " .. (d.ftl_range_ly or "?") .. " ly",
		"Power: " .. (d.power or "?"),
		"Sensor Range: " .. (d.sensor_range_m or "?") .. " m",
		"",
		"Slots: W" .. (c.W or 0) .. "  C" .. (c.C or 0) .. "  E" .. (c.E or 0) .. "  H" .. (c.H or 0)
			.. (session.is_ship_advanced(ship_id) and "  (Advanced)" or ""),
	}
	return table.concat(lines, "\n")
end

-- Refreshes the Fitting tab's Ship Statistics panel (main/outpost.gui's
-- ship_stats_content) - called on every Fitting-tab (re)entry and loadout
-- change (from refresh_markers, since that's already the single place
-- that re-syncs visual state with session.get_loadout()), and with a live
-- `highlight` while a drag hovers a vacant matching slot (from
-- update_hover) or with none when that hover ends (clear_hover).
local function refresh_ship_stats(self, highlight)
	gui.set_text(self.ship_stats_content_node, ship_stats_text(session.get_ship_id(), highlight))
end

-- The system whose outpost the player is at (session.docked_system(): their
-- current system if their faction has an outpost there, otherwise home -
-- the same rule session.launch() uses for where the next flight starts).
local function docked_system_id()
	return session.docked_system()
end

local function system_name(system_id)
	return (system_id and star_systems.SYSTEMS[system_id] and star_systems.SYSTEMS[system_id].name) or "?"
end

-- Outpost screen header: the docked system's name, faction, active ship.
local function ship_overview_text(faction)
	local ship_name = ship_display_name(session.get_ship_id(), faction)
	return system_name(docked_system_id()), FACTION_DISPLAY[faction] or faction, ship_name
end

-- Keeps the always-visible Scrip/Water/Iron/Hydrogen readouts in sync with
-- session state - called on show_outpost and after every
-- confirmation-dialog resolution (buy/sell/swap/switch), since those are
-- the only things that ever change the Scrip balance (plan.md §4/§2.6).
-- Water/Iron/Hydrogen have no earning/spending mechanic wired up anywhere
-- yet (session.lua's own comment on M.water/M.iron/M.hydrogen), so those
-- three will just keep re-displaying the same static starting value every
-- call for now - still routed through the same refresh so they start
-- updating for free the moment a real mechanic sets them.
local function refresh_currency(self)
	gui.set_text(self.scrip_node, "Scrip: " .. session.get_scrip())
	gui.set_text(self.water_node, "Water: " .. session.get_water())
	gui.set_text(self.iron_node, "Iron: " .. session.get_iron())
	gui.set_text(self.hydrogen_node, "Hydrogen: " .. session.get_hydrogen())
end

-- Order/display names for the fitting-completeness breakdown below -
-- same four slot types §2.8's module system already uses.
local SLOT_TYPE_ORDER = { "W", "C", "E", "H" }
local SLOT_TYPE_LABEL = { W = "Weapon", C = "Computer", E = "Engine", H = "Hull" }

-- Collection name of `skin_id`, or "Default" for nil (no skin equipped).
local function skin_display_name(skin_id)
	local skin = skin_id and skins.SKINS[skin_id]
	local collection = skin and skin_collections.COLLECTIONS[skin.collection]
	return collection and collection.name or "Default"
end

-- Builds the Overview tab's whole status readout as one multi-line
-- string - real data pulled straight from session/ships/catalog, not an
-- invented "progress %" or rank/XP system that doesn't actually exist
-- anywhere in this project (plan.md §0/§4's "don't invent unconfirmed
-- numbers" rule extends to not inventing progression systems either).
-- Today's assignments as last fetched from the server (network.assignments,
-- on every show_outpost), for the Overview; nil until the first reply.
local assignments_cache = nil

local function overview_text()
	local faction = session.get_faction()
	local faction_label = FACTION_DISPLAY[faction] or faction
	local home_id = star_systems.HOME_SYSTEM[faction]
	local home_name = (home_id and star_systems.SYSTEMS[home_id].name) or "?"

	local active_ship_id = session.get_ship_id()
	local active_ship = ships.SHIPS[active_ship_id]
	local active_ship_name = ship_display_name(active_ship_id, faction)
	local active_ship_class = active_ship and active_ship.class or "?"

	local owned_ship_ids = session.get_owned_ships() or {}
	local owned_ship_names = {}
	for _, ship_id in ipairs(owned_ship_ids) do
		table.insert(owned_ship_names, ship_display_name(ship_id, faction))
	end

	-- Fitting completeness for the ACTIVE ship: filled vs. total slots,
	-- broken down by type - straight from ships.lua's `components` (or
	-- `advanced`, once upgraded - effective_components picks whichever
	-- applies) and session.get_loadout() (what's actually filled).
	local components = effective_components(active_ship_id)
	local filled_by_type = {}
	for _, entry in ipairs(session.get_loadout() or {}) do
		local prefix = entry.slot:sub(1, 1)
		filled_by_type[prefix] = (filled_by_type[prefix] or 0) + 1
	end
	local total_slots, filled_slots = 0, 0
	local fit_lines = {}
	for _, slot_type in ipairs(SLOT_TYPE_ORDER) do
		local total = components[slot_type] or 0
		if total > 0 then
			local filled = filled_by_type[slot_type] or 0
			total_slots = total_slots + total
			filled_slots = filled_slots + filled
			table.insert(fit_lines, "    " .. SLOT_TYPE_LABEL[slot_type] .. ": " .. filled .. "/" .. total)
		end
	end

	-- Inventory: every owned module instance, spare vs. installed, plus
	-- how many upgrade levels have been applied in total (§2.8.2) - a
	-- real, honest tally, not a fabricated score.
	local owned_instances = session.get_owned() or {}
	local installed_count, spare_count, total_levels = 0, 0, 0
	for _, inst in ipairs(owned_instances) do
		if session.is_installed(inst.id) then
			installed_count = installed_count + 1
		else
			spare_count = spare_count + 1
		end
		total_levels = total_levels + (inst.level or 0)
	end

	local level = session.get_level()
	local lines = {
		"Faction: " .. faction_label,
		"Rank: " .. (session.get_rank() or "?") .. "  (level " .. level .. ")",
		"XP: " .. with_commas(session.get_xp()) .. "  (level " .. (level + 1) .. " at " .. with_commas(ranks.xp_for_level(level + 1)) .. ")",
		"Home system: " .. home_name,
		"Docked at: " .. system_name(docked_system_id()) .. " outpost",
		"",
		"Active ship: " .. active_ship_name .. " (" .. active_ship_class .. ")"
			.. (session.is_ship_advanced(active_ship_id) and "  [Advanced]" or ""),
		"Skin: " .. skin_display_name(session.get_equipped_skin(active_ship_id)),
		"Fleet: " .. #owned_ship_ids .. (#owned_ship_ids == 1 and " ship" or " ships")
			.. (#owned_ship_names > 0 and ("  --  " .. table.concat(owned_ship_names, ", ")) or ""),
		"",
		"Active ship fitting: " .. filled_slots .. "/" .. total_slots .. " slots filled",
	}
	for _, line in ipairs(fit_lines) do
		table.insert(lines, line)
	end
	table.insert(lines, "")
	table.insert(lines, "Components owned: " .. #owned_instances
		.. "  (" .. installed_count .. " installed, " .. spare_count .. " spare)")
	table.insert(lines, "Total upgrades applied: " .. total_levels)

	if assignments_cache and assignments_cache.list then
		table.insert(lines, "")
		table.insert(lines, "Today's assignments:")
		for _, a in ipairs(assignments_cache.list) do
			local state = a.done and "done" or (with_commas(a.progress) .. "/" .. with_commas(a.target))
			table.insert(lines, "    " .. a.name .. ": " .. state .. "  --  " .. a.description
				.. (a.done and "" or ("  (+" .. with_commas(a.xp) .. " XP, +" .. with_commas(a.scrip) .. " Scrip)")))
		end
	end

	return table.concat(lines, "\n")
end

local function refresh_overview(self)
	gui.set_text(self.overview_content_node, overview_text())
end

-- Octagon-shaped node (per direct instruction: slot markers should be
-- octagons, not rectangles) - built from Defold's native TYPE_PIE node
-- rather than a custom texture: an 8-vertex pie with a full 360-degree
-- fill and no inner radius renders as a plain regular octagon inscribed
-- in `size`'s bounding box, no image asset needed. gui.pick_node's hit
-- test is bounding-box based regardless of node type/shape, so drag/drop
-- hit-testing against the marker is unaffected by the corners being
-- visually cut off.
-- NOTE: the Lua API is gui.set_fill_angle, NOT gui.set_pie_fill_angle -
-- confirmed against the official API docs (defold.com/ref/stable/gui) -
-- despite the underlying .gui file's design-time field actually being
-- named `pie_fill_angle` (confirmed separately by inspecting bob.jar's
-- compiled NodeDesc protobuf class), which is what led to the wrong
-- guess the first time around. That earlier wrong name was a real bug,
-- not just a caveat: calling a nil `gui.set_pie_fill_angle` throws a Lua
-- runtime error, which aborts build_markers() entirely partway through
-- its first iteration - only the very first slot's `border` node had
-- been created (via new_pie_node + the two setter calls before the
-- crash) and was never reached by its own set_enabled(false) (that line
-- runs in build_markers, AFTER new_octagon_node returns - which it never
-- did), leaving one always-visible, default-white, BORDER_SIZE-shaped
-- octagon on screen and none of the other 9 slots' markers ever built.
local function new_octagon_node(pos, size)
	local node = gui.new_pie_node(pos, size)
	gui.set_perimeter_vertices(node, 8)
	gui.set_outer_bounds(node, gui.PIEBOUNDS_ELLIPSE)
	gui.set_fill_angle(node, 360)
	gui.set_inner_radius(node, 0)
	-- With 8 perimeter vertices evenly spaced around the ellipse, Defold
	-- places the first vertex at 0 degrees (3 o'clock) by default, which
	-- puts a VERTEX (a point) at the top/bottom/left/right and a flat edge
	-- on each diagonal - the opposite of what was asked for. Rotating the
	-- whole node 22.5 degrees (half of the 45-degree gap between the 8
	-- vertices) shifts the vertices onto the diagonals instead, so a flat
	-- edge ends up centered on the top/bottom/left/right instead (per
	-- direct instruction).
	gui.set_rotation(node, vmath.vector3(0, 0, 22.5))
	return node
end

local function clear_markers(self)
	for _, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		gui.delete_node(marker.border)
		gui.delete_node(marker.box)
		gui.delete_node(marker.icon)
		gui.delete_node(marker.label)
		gui.delete_node(marker.level_label)
		gui.delete_node(marker.hotkey_label)
		gui.delete_node(marker.invalid_mark)
	end
	self.markers = {}
end

-- Builds one marker per entry in the current ship's `slot_positions`,
-- placed at ship_visual's own screen position plus that slot's stored
-- {x, y} offset (screen-pixel units, center-origin — see
-- main/data/ships.lua's own comment on slot_positions). `box`/`border` are
-- octagons (new_octagon_node), not plain rectangles, per direct
-- instruction. Creation order matters for draw order: border (behind) ->
-- box -> icon -> label (a small slot-id tag, top of the box) ->
-- level_label (a small upgrade-level badge, bottom-right corner — blank
-- unless the installed instance is upgraded, §4's per-instance upgrade
-- decision) -> invalid_mark (in front of everything).
-- Shows the active ship's own top-down silhouette under the slot markers.
-- Rendered by tools/render_topdown_silhouettes.py into main/images/topdown/
-- (bundled via game.project's custom_resources, not an atlas), named after
-- the faction skin's .model file, at the same 440 x 640 size as
-- `ship_visual` itself, so slot_positions' pixel offsets line up with it.
-- Only the one image on screen is loaded; the previous one is freed.
local function show_ship_silhouette(self, ship)
	local skin = ship and ship.faction_skins and ship.faction_skins[session.get_faction()]
	local name = skin and skin.model and skin.model:match("([^/]+)%.model$")
	if not name or name == self.silhouette_name then
		return
	end
	local png = sys.load_resource("/main/images/topdown/" .. name .. ".png")
	local img = png and image.load(png, { premultiply_alpha = true })
	if not img then
		print("[outpost] no top-down silhouette for " .. name)
		return
	end
	local texture_id = "silhouette_" .. name
	if not gui.new_texture(texture_id, img.width, img.height, img.type, img.buffer, false) then
		return
	end
	gui.set_texture(self.ship_visual_node, texture_id)
	if self.silhouette_name then
		gui.delete_texture("silhouette_" .. self.silhouette_name)
	end
	self.silhouette_name = name
end

local function build_markers(self)
	clear_markers(self)
	local ship_id = session.get_ship_id()
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
	show_ship_silhouette(self, ship)
	local positions = ship and ship.slot_positions or {}
	local counts = effective_components(ship_id)
	local visual_pos = gui.get_position(self.ship_visual_node)
	for slot_id, offset in pairs(positions) do
		-- Advanced-only slots (direct instruction: e.g. Patrol Interceptor's
		-- W4/E5) stay defined in slot_positions for BOTH tiers, but only
		-- actually USABLE once the ship's been advanced - still shown (not
		-- hidden), just marked `locked` below and rendered greyed-out,
		-- non-interactive (refresh_markers/update_hover/the drop-resolution
		-- in on_input all check this) until then. A slot's ordinal number
		-- exceeding the CURRENT tier's count for that type
		-- (effective_components already picks `advanced` vs `components`)
		-- is what makes it locked.
		local prefix, number = slot_id:match("^(%a)(%d+)$")
		local slot_total = prefix and counts[prefix]
		local locked = slot_total ~= nil and tonumber(number) > slot_total
		local pos = vmath.vector3(visual_pos.x + offset.x, visual_pos.y + offset.y, 0)

		local border = new_octagon_node(pos, BORDER_SIZE)
		gui.set_pivot(border, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_enabled(border, false)

		local box = new_octagon_node(pos, MARKER_SIZE)
		gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)

		local icon = gui.new_box_node(pos, ICON_SIZE)
		gui.set_pivot(icon, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_texture(icon, ICON_TEXTURE)
		-- The component icon must render at exactly ICON_SIZE regardless of
		-- the assigned texture's own pixel dimensions/aspect ratio -
		-- explicitly forced to MANUAL rather than left to default, since the
		-- hand-provided "Octagon *" icons (§2.8.1) are much higher-resolution
		-- than the old 128x128 procedurally-generated placeholders and, for
		-- the two Cannon icons, not even square - AUTO size mode would both
		-- inflate the node past ICON_SIZE and distort the marker layout
		-- around it.
		gui.set_size_mode(icon, gui.SIZE_MODE_MANUAL)

		-- Small slot-id tag (e.g. "W1"), pinned to the top edge of the
		-- marker box so it doesn't overlap the icon at its center.
		local label_pos = vmath.vector3(pos.x, pos.y + MARKER_SIZE.y / 2 - 10, 0)
		local label = gui.new_text_node(label_pos, slot_id)
		gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(label, vmath.vector4(0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 1.0))
		gui.set_scale(label, vmath.vector3(0.5, 0.5, 1))

		-- Small upgrade-level badge (e.g. "+1"), bottom-right corner —
		-- text set/cleared in refresh_markers, blank when empty or at
		-- level 0.
		local level_label_pos = vmath.vector3(pos.x + MARKER_SIZE.x / 2 - 14, pos.y - MARKER_SIZE.y / 2 + 10, 0)
		local level_label = gui.new_text_node(level_label_pos, "")
		gui.set_pivot(level_label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(level_label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(level_label, vmath.vector4(1.0, 0.85, 0.3, 1.0))
		gui.set_scale(level_label, vmath.vector3(0.5, 0.5, 1))

		-- The key that uses this slot in flight (session.slot_hotkey):
		-- "Shift+1" under a fitted weapon, "Key 3" under an active module -
		-- text set in refresh_markers, blank when nothing usable is fitted.
		local hotkey_label = gui.new_text_node(vmath.vector3(pos.x, pos.y - MARKER_SIZE.y / 2 - 18, 0), "")
		gui.set_pivot(hotkey_label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(hotkey_label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(hotkey_label, HOTKEY_LABEL_COLOR)
		gui.set_scale(hotkey_label, vmath.vector3(0.85, 0.85, 1))

		local invalid_mark = gui.new_text_node(pos, "X")
		gui.set_pivot(invalid_mark, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(invalid_mark, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(invalid_mark, INVALID_MARK_COLOR)
		gui.set_scale(invalid_mark, vmath.vector3(3, 3, 1))
		gui.set_enabled(invalid_mark, false)

		self.markers[slot_id] = {
			box = box, icon = icon, label = label, level_label = level_label, hotkey_label = hotkey_label,
			border = border, invalid_mark = invalid_mark,
			type = SLOT_TYPE_BY_PREFIX[slot_id:sub(1, 1)],
			locked = locked,
		}
	end
end

-- Hides every slot's hover-highlight (border + invalid mark) — called
-- whenever there's no drag in progress, or once a drag ends.
local function clear_hover(self)
	for _, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		gui.set_enabled(marker.border, false)
		gui.set_enabled(marker.invalid_mark, false)
	end
	self.hovered_slot = nil
	refresh_ship_stats(self) -- drop any hover-preview highlight, back to the ship's real stats
end

-- Drag-over feedback: highlights whichever slot the cursor is currently
-- over with a neon border — cyan if the dragged item's type fits that
-- slot, red (plus a red X) if it doesn't. Called continuously while
-- dragging, from on_input's cursor-tracking branch.
local function update_hover(self, action)
	local hit_slot = nil
	for slot_id, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		if gui.pick_node(marker.box, action.x, action.y) then
			hit_slot = slot_id
			break
		end
	end
	local stats_highlight = nil
	for slot_id, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		if slot_id == hit_slot then
			-- Locked (advanced-only, not unlocked yet) slots are never a
			-- valid drop target, same red-border-plus-X feedback as a
			-- type mismatch (direct instruction: greyed out AND unusable).
			local valid = marker.type == self.dragging.type and not marker.locked
			gui.set_enabled(marker.border, true)
			gui.set_color(marker.border, valid and BORDER_VALID_COLOR or BORDER_INVALID_COLOR)
			gui.set_enabled(marker.invalid_mark, not valid)
			-- Stats hover-preview (per direct instruction: "when a
			-- component is hovered over a vacant slot show the revised
			-- statistics") - VACANT only, not a swap: a swap's "revised"
			-- stats would need to subtract the DISPLACED module's own
			-- stats too, which none of these modules have a numeric
			-- amount for anyway yet (see ship_stats_text's own comment) -
			-- out of scope for now, see plan.md §4.
			local occupied = self.installed_by_slot and self.installed_by_slot[slot_id]
			if valid and not occupied then
				local module = catalog.get(self.dragging.item_key)
				if module and module.stats then
					stats_highlight = {}
					for _, stat in ipairs(module.stats) do
						stats_highlight[stat] = true
					end
				end
			end
		else
			gui.set_enabled(marker.border, false)
			gui.set_enabled(marker.invalid_mark, false)
		end
	end
	self.hovered_slot = hit_slot
	refresh_ship_stats(self, stats_highlight)
end

-- Recolors every marker and swaps its icon from session.get_loadout()'s
-- current state (called after any install/uninstall, and whenever the
-- Fitting tab (re)opens). The slot-id label (e.g. "W1") never changes;
-- the icon shows the installed INSTANCE's module icon when filled, or a
-- dim generic outline icon for that slot's type when empty — replacing
-- the old "show the item's name as text" approach (plan.md §4). The
-- level badge shows that instance's upgrade level (§4's per-instance
-- upgrade decision) when it's above 0, and is blank otherwise.
local function refresh_markers(self)
	local installed_by_slot = {}
	for _, entry in ipairs(session.get_loadout() or {}) do
		installed_by_slot[entry.slot] = entry.instance_id
	end
	-- Cached for update_weapon_arc_hover below, so the hover check (run on
	-- every mouse-move) doesn't need to rebuild this from session.get_loadout()
	-- itself each time - refreshed here alongside the markers it describes.
	self.installed_by_slot = installed_by_slot
	for slot_id, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		local instance_id = installed_by_slot[slot_id]
		local inst = instance_id and session.get_instance(instance_id)
		local module = inst and catalog.get(inst.item_key)
		local icon_id = (module and module.icon) or SLOT_EMPTY_ICON[marker.type]
		gui.play_flipbook(marker.icon, icon_id)
		if marker.locked then
			-- Advanced-only slot, ship not advanced yet (direct instruction:
			-- greyed out and unusable, not hidden) - a locked slot is always
			-- empty (nothing can ever be installed into it while locked), so
			-- there's no filled/level-badge state to consider here.
			gui.set_color(marker.box, SLOT_LOCKED_COLOR)
			gui.set_color(marker.icon, SLOT_LOCKED_ICON_COLOR)
		else
			gui.set_color(marker.box, inst and SLOT_FILLED_COLOR or SLOT_EMPTY_COLOR)
			gui.set_color(marker.icon, vmath.vector4(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0))
		end
		gui.set_text(marker.level_label, (inst and inst.level > 0) and ("+" .. inst.level) or "")
		local key = module and session.slot_hotkey(session.get_ship_id(), slot_id)
		local hotkey_text = ""
		if key and marker.type == "weapon" then
			hotkey_text = "Shift+" .. key
		elseif key and module.behavior == "active" then
			hotkey_text = "Key " .. key
		end
		gui.set_text(marker.hotkey_label, hotkey_text)
	end
	refresh_ship_stats(self) -- keep the Ship Statistics panel in sync with whatever just changed
end

local function hide_weapon_arc(self)
	gui.set_enabled(self.weapon_arc_node, false)
	self.weapon_arc_hover_slot = nil
end

-- Shows the firing-arc wedge for one occupied weapon slot, centered on that
-- slot's own mount point (same {x, y} the marker itself sits at) and aimed
-- per ships.lua's `angle_deg` (plan.md §2.8) - not the ship's overall center,
-- since different weapon slots on the same hull can point different ways
-- (e.g. Patrol Interceptor's W2/W3 angled +-15 off the bow).
--
-- Defold's TYPE_PIE fill starts at local 0 degrees (3 o'clock - see
-- new_octagon_node's own comment on perimeter-vertex placement) and sweeps
-- toward increasing angles; node `rotation` then offsets that start point.
-- Converting our own bearing convention (0 = dead ahead/+y, positive =
-- clockwise toward +x) into the pie's math-angle space (0 = +x/east,
-- positive = counter-clockwise) gives `90 - angle_deg` as the wedge's
-- CENTER in pie-space - offsetting by half the arc width then gives the
-- rotation needed to make the pie's fill START there, so the fill ends up
-- centered on the intended bearing rather than starting from it.
local function show_weapon_arc(self, slot_id, angle_deg, arc)
	local marker = self.markers[slot_id]
	if not marker then return end
	local pos = gui.get_position(marker.box)
	gui.set_position(self.weapon_arc_node, pos)
	gui.set_size(self.weapon_arc_node, WEAPON_ARC_SIZE)
	gui.set_fill_angle(self.weapon_arc_node, arc)
	gui.set_rotation(self.weapon_arc_node, vmath.vector3(0, 0, 90 - angle_deg - arc / 2))
	gui.set_enabled(self.weapon_arc_node, true)
	self.weapon_arc_hover_slot = slot_id
end

-- Mouse-move hover check for the wedge above - separate from update_hover
-- (which only runs mid-drag, for the drag-over slot highlight); this one
-- runs whenever the Fitting tab is open and nothing else has input focus,
-- regardless of dragging. Only occupied, unlocked WEAPON slots show
-- anything - hull/engine/computer slots don't fire, so they never have an
-- `angle_deg`/`arc` to show in the first place.
local function update_weapon_arc_hover(self, action)
	local ship_id = session.get_ship_id()
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
	local positions = ship and ship.slot_positions or {}
	for slot_id, marker in pairs(self.markers) do
		if marker.type == "weapon" and not marker.locked and gui.pick_node(marker.box, action.x, action.y) then
			local instance_id = self.installed_by_slot and self.installed_by_slot[slot_id]
			local inst = instance_id and session.get_instance(instance_id)
			local module = inst and catalog.get(inst.item_key)
			if module and module.arc then
				local slot_pos = positions[slot_id]
				show_weapon_arc(self, slot_id, (slot_pos and slot_pos.angle_deg) or 0, module.arc)
				return
			end
			hide_weapon_arc(self)
			return
		end
	end
	hide_weapon_arc(self)
end

local function clear_card_list(list)
	for _, card in ipairs(list) do
		-- card.box is nil for the "(none)" empty-list placeholder (see
		-- build_card_list) - only a label exists for that one.
		if card.box then
			gui.delete_node(card.box)
		end
		if card.icon then
			gui.delete_node(card.icon)
		end
		gui.delete_node(card.label)
		-- sell_box/sell_label only exist on sellable rows (Owned spares,
		-- Owned Ships) - see build_card_list.
		if card.sell_box then
			gui.delete_node(card.sell_box)
			gui.delete_node(card.sell_label)
		end
		-- strip only exists on Ships-For-Sale grid cells (the name+price
		-- backing bar drawn on top of the live 3D preview) - see
		-- build_ship_forsale_grid.
		if card.strip then
			gui.delete_node(card.strip)
		end
		if card.detail then
			gui.delete_node(card.detail)
		end
	end
end

-- The active ship's CLASS (e.g. "Patrol"), not the ship itself - what
-- module_fits_class below actually filters against.
local function active_ship_class()
	local ship = ships.SHIPS[session.get_ship_id()]
	return ship and ship.class
end

-- Does `module` fit `ship_class`? Per direct instruction: the Shop and
-- Owned lists should only ever offer weapons/components that actually
-- fit the ACTIVE ship's class - this is the enforcement that was
-- flagged as missing in plan.md §4 ever since ship_class/ship_classes
-- were added as pure data with nothing reading them yet. Handles both
-- schemas currently in use: the singular `ship_class` string (decided
-- for auto cannons, §2.8 - a module belongs to exactly one class) and
-- the older plural `ship_classes` list (still used by other module
-- types, e.g. the Asteroid Analyser, which "fits anywhere for now").
-- Falls back to "fits anywhere" for a module with neither field, rather
-- than silently hiding it for missing data.
local function module_fits_class(module, ship_class)
	if not module then
		return false
	end
	if module.ship_class then
		return module.ship_class == ship_class
	end
	if module.ship_classes then
		for _, c in ipairs(module.ship_classes) do
			if c == ship_class then
				return true
			end
		end
		return false
	end
	return true
end

-- Every owned instance NOT currently installed anywhere AND that fits
-- the active ship's class — i.e. the Owned (spares) list. Two spare
-- copies of the same module type each get their own row/card here
-- (they're separate instances, possibly at different upgrade levels,
-- §4), unlike the old type-key-only model where owning a second copy of
-- something already-owned was indistinguishable from the first.
-- `type_filter` (optional - nil/"weapon"/"computer"/"engine"/"hull", §2.8.8)
-- additionally restricts to modules of exactly that type, on top of the
-- existing ship_class fit check - the Fitting tab's type-filter buttons.
local function owned_spare_instances(type_filter)
	local ship_class = active_ship_class()
	local instances = {}
	for _, inst in ipairs(session.get_owned() or {}) do
		local module = catalog.get(inst.item_key)
		if not session.is_installed(inst.id) and module_fits_class(module, ship_class)
			and (not type_filter or (module and module.type == type_filter)) then
			table.insert(instances, inst)
		end
	end
	return instances
end

-- Every catalog entry that fits the active ship's class - unlike the old
-- model, the Shop no longer hides an item once you own one copy of it:
-- purchasing always grants a brand-new INSTANCE (session.purchase, §4),
-- so buying a second (or third) Gnat to fit and upgrade independently
-- needs to still be possible. It DOES now hide items that belong to a
-- different ship class entirely (e.g. Miner/Speculator/Prospector while
-- flying the Patrol-class Sardine/Hummingbird), per direct instruction.
-- `type_filter`: see owned_spare_instances above.
local function shop_keys(type_filter)
	local ship_class = active_ship_class()
	local keys = {}
	for item_key, module in pairs(catalog.ALL) do
		if module_fits_class(module, ship_class) and (not type_filter or module.type == type_filter) then
			table.insert(keys, item_key)
		end
	end
	table.sort(keys)
	return keys
end

-- How far a list of `row_count` items is allowed to scroll (pixels),
-- given the shared PANEL_HEIGHT every card-list panel uses. 0 once
-- everything already fits without scrolling.
local function max_scroll_for(row_count)
	if row_count <= 1 then
		return 0
	end
	local content_height = (row_count - 1) * CARD_SPACING + CARD_SIZE.y
	return math.max(0, content_height - PANEL_HEIGHT)
end

-- Fills one list panel with clickable/draggable cards, top-down, same
-- dynamic node-creation pattern as the reference project's own
-- component/ship buttons. `rows` is a list of already-fully-resolved
-- { item_key, instance_id (optional - nil for a Shop/type-only row, or a
-- ship_id row), label, color (optional box override), source } tables -
-- built by the caller (shop_rows/owned_rows/ship_*_rows below), so this
-- helper doesn't need to know anything about catalog/session/ships
-- itself. Generalized like this so the same helper builds both the
-- Fitting tab's module cards and the Ships tab's ship cards. `list_source`
-- tags the empty-list "(none)" placeholder card (rows already carry their
-- own `source` per-row, but there's no row to pull one from when the list
-- is empty) - callers checking `card.source == "ship_forsale"` etc. still
-- need it set even on a placeholder, so an empty For Sale list is still
-- recognizably a For Sale card and not just an untagged blank.
--
-- `scroll_offset` (pixels, 0 = scrolled to the top) shifts every row up
-- by that amount before the panel's own clipping (set once in init(),
-- CLIPPING_MODE_STENCIL) hides whatever's scrolled past its edge - so a
-- list longer than PANEL_HEIGHT scrolls instead of overflowing into
-- whatever's below it (plan.md §4). Every card gets reparented under
-- `panel_node` (keep_scene_transform=true, so it stays exactly where it
-- was just positioned) purely so it inherits that clipping - cards
-- aren't otherwise logically owned by the panel node.
local function build_card_list(out_list, panel_node, rows, list_source, scroll_offset)
	scroll_offset = scroll_offset or 0
	local panel_pos = gui.get_position(panel_node)
	local panel_size = gui.get_size(panel_node)
	local top = panel_pos.y + panel_size.y * 0.5 - 35 + scroll_offset
	if #rows == 0 then
		local pos = vmath.vector3(panel_pos.x, top, 0)
		local label = gui.new_text_node(pos, "(none)")
		gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(label, vmath.vector4(0.5, 0.55, 0.6, 1.0))
		gui.set_parent(label, panel_node, true)
		table.insert(out_list, { box = nil, label = label, item_key = nil, instance_id = nil, source = list_source })
		return
	end
	for i, row in ipairs(rows) do
		local pos = vmath.vector3(panel_pos.x, top - (i - 1) * CARD_SPACING, 0)
		local box = gui.new_box_node(pos, CARD_SIZE)
		gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(box, row.color or CARD_COLOR)
		gui.set_parent(box, panel_node, true)

		-- Module icon (direct instruction: the Shop/Owned lists should show
		-- each module's icon next to its name, not just text) - same
		-- per-type "Octagon" fallback SLOT_EMPTY_ICON already uses for an
		-- empty fitting slot, so a row without its own catalog icon still
		-- shows something meaningful for its type rather than nothing.
		local icon
		local module_for_icon = row.item_key and catalog.get(row.item_key)
		if module_for_icon then
			local icon_pos = vmath.vector3(pos.x - CARD_SIZE.x / 2 + ROW_ICON_MARGIN + ROW_ICON_SIZE.x / 2, pos.y, 0)
			icon = gui.new_box_node(icon_pos, ROW_ICON_SIZE)
			gui.set_pivot(icon, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_texture(icon, ICON_TEXTURE)
			gui.set_size_mode(icon, gui.SIZE_MODE_MANUAL)
			gui.play_flipbook(icon, module_for_icon.icon or SLOT_EMPTY_ICON[module_for_icon.type])
			gui.set_parent(icon, panel_node, true)
		end

		-- Sellable rows (Owned spares, Owned Ships, §2.8/§4) shift their
		-- own label left to make room for a "Sell" button pinned to the
		-- right edge, so the two never visually overlap. Left-pivoted and
		-- positioned right after the icon (direct instruction: icon next to
		-- the name, not just floating at the card's own left edge
		-- separately) rather than the old card-wide centered label.
		local label_x = pos.x - CARD_SIZE.x / 2 + ROW_ICON_MARGIN + (icon and (ROW_ICON_SIZE.x + ROW_ICON_GAP) or 0)
		local label = gui.new_text_node(vmath.vector3(label_x, pos.y, 0), row.label)
		gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_W)
		gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(label, vmath.vector4(0.9, 0.95, 1.0, 1.0))
		gui.set_parent(label, panel_node, true)

		local sell_box, sell_label
		if row.sellable then
			local sell_pos = vmath.vector3(pos.x + CARD_SIZE.x / 2 - SELL_BUTTON_SIZE.x / 2 - 10, pos.y, 0)
			sell_box = gui.new_box_node(sell_pos, SELL_BUTTON_SIZE)
			gui.set_pivot(sell_box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_color(sell_box, SELL_BUTTON_COLOR)
			gui.set_parent(sell_box, panel_node, true)
			sell_label = gui.new_text_node(sell_pos, "Sell")
			gui.set_pivot(sell_label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_font(sell_label, "default_font")
			gui.set_color(sell_label, vmath.vector4(1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0))
			gui.set_scale(sell_label, vmath.vector3(0.7, 0.7, 1))
			gui.set_parent(sell_label, panel_node, true)
		end

		table.insert(out_list, {
			box = box, label = label, icon = icon,
			item_key = row.item_key, instance_id = row.instance_id, source = row.source,
			sell_box = sell_box, sell_label = sell_label,
		})
	end
end

-- `type_filter`: see owned_spare_instances above.
local function shop_rows(type_filter)
	local rows = {}
	for _, item_key in ipairs(shop_keys(type_filter)) do
		local module = catalog.get(item_key)
		table.insert(rows, {
			item_key = item_key,
			label = (module and module.name or item_key) .. "  (" .. session.get_module_price() .. " Scrip)",
			source = "shop",
			color = module and TYPE_CARD_COLOR[module.type],
		})
	end
	return rows
end

local function owned_spare_rows(type_filter)
	local rows = {}
	for _, inst in ipairs(owned_spare_instances(type_filter)) do
		local module = catalog.get(inst.item_key)
		local name = module and module.name or inst.item_key
		if inst.level > 0 then
			name = name .. "  +" .. inst.level
		end
		table.insert(rows, {
			item_key = inst.item_key, instance_id = inst.id,
			label = name, source = "owned", sellable = true, -- §2.8/§4: components can be sold
			color = module and TYPE_CARD_COLOR[module.type],
		})
	end
	return rows
end

-- Recolors the four type-filter buttons: bright FILTER_ACTIVE_COLOR for
-- whichever type is currently selected (self.type_filter), dim
-- TYPE_CARD_COLOR (matching the unfiltered card color) for the rest, same
-- "active vs. inactive" pattern as the main Overview/Fitting/Ships tab bar.
local function refresh_filter_buttons(self)
	for _, type_name in ipairs(FILTER_TYPE_ORDER) do
		local active = self.type_filter == type_name
		gui.set_color(self.filter_nodes[type_name], active and FILTER_ACTIVE_COLOR[type_name] or TYPE_CARD_COLOR[type_name])
	end
end

local function refresh_module_cards(self)
	clear_card_list(self.cards)
	self.cards = {}
	local shop = shop_rows(self.type_filter)
	local owned = owned_spare_rows(self.type_filter)
	self.scroll.shop = math.min(self.scroll.shop, max_scroll_for(#shop))
	self.scroll.owned = math.min(self.scroll.owned, max_scroll_for(#owned))
	build_card_list(self.cards, self.shop_panel_node, shop, "shop", self.scroll.shop)
	build_card_list(self.cards, self.owned_panel_node, owned, "owned", self.scroll.owned)
end

-- Every named ship in the roster, owned or not (direct instruction: the
-- Ships grid now shows the whole class/role matrix at once - an owned hull
-- just renders highlighted in place rather than being pulled into a
-- separate list, see ship_forsale_rows below).
local function ship_forsale_keys()
	local keys = {}
	for ship_id, _ in pairs(ships.SHIPS) do
		table.insert(keys, ship_id)
	end
	table.sort(keys)
	return keys
end

-- `active_ship`: session.get_ship_id(), needed here (not just in the modal)
-- so the currently-flown hull's cell gets its own distinct highlight -
-- SHIP_CARD_CURRENT_COLOR for "this is what you're flying", the cooler
-- SHIP_CARD_OWNED_COLOR for "you own this, click to swap to it", or no
-- override (falls back to build_ship_forsale_grid's own CARD_COLOR) for a
-- hull that's still purchasable. `sellable` mirrors the old Owned Ships
-- list's "can't sell your last ship" rule - session.sell_ship itself also
-- refuses to go below one, this is just the UI-level courtesy on top.
local function ship_forsale_rows(faction, active_ship)
	local owned_count = #(session.get_owned_ships() or {})
	local rows = {}
	for _, ship_id in ipairs(ship_forsale_keys()) do
		local ship = ships.SHIPS[ship_id]
		local skin = ship and faction and ship.faction_skins[faction]
		local owned = session.is_ship_owned(ship_id)
		local is_active = ship_id == active_ship
		local name = ship_display_name(ship_id, faction)
		local advanced_tag = session.is_ship_advanced(ship_id) and "  [Advanced]" or ""
		local label, detail, color = name, nil, nil
		if is_active then
			detail = "Current" .. advanced_tag
			color = SHIP_CARD_CURRENT_COLOR
		elseif owned then
			detail = "Owned" .. advanced_tag
			color = SHIP_CARD_OWNED_COLOR
		elseif session.get_level() < session.ship_level_required(ship_id) then
			detail = "Level " .. session.ship_level_required(ship_id) .. " required"
		else
			detail = ship_price_text(ship_id)
		end
		table.insert(rows, {
			item_key = ship_id,
			label = label,
			detail = detail,
			color = color,
			source = "ship_forsale",
			-- Carried through to build_ship_forsale_grid's own card entries
			-- so compute_forsale_preview_items doesn't need to re-derive
			-- ship/faction lookups itself - "<TBD>" (no real asset yet)
			-- naturally fails PREVIEW_WORLD_POS's own lookup later, same
			-- honest "no preview available" fallback the ship detail modal
			-- already uses.
			model_path = skin and skin.model,
			owned = owned,
			sellable = owned and owned_count > 1,
		})
	end
	return rows
end

-- Grid version of max_scroll_for above, for the Ships-For-Sale panel only -
-- a fixed #CLASS_ORDER rows regardless of how many ships are actually for
-- sale right now (each class always occupies its own row, even with an
-- empty cell or two where an owned ship's column would be).
local function max_scroll_for_grid()
	local grid_rows = #CLASS_ORDER
	if grid_rows <= 1 then
		return 0
	end
	local content_height = (grid_rows - 1) * (GRID_CELL_H + GRID_GUTTER) + GRID_CELL_H
	return math.max(0, content_height - SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT)
end

-- Grid variant of build_card_list, used ONLY for the Ships-For-Sale panel
-- (direct instruction: grid layout with a live 3D preview per cell instead
-- of the old single-column text list). Each cell's `box` is both the click
-- hit-target (same role as every other card's box) AND the area the render
-- script's live 3D overlay paints into - this function only creates that
-- box plus a name+price text strip along its bottom edge; it doesn't touch
-- the render script itself. `model_path` is carried onto each card entry so
-- compute_forsale_preview_items (below, near show_ship_detail) can build the
-- render script's preview list straight from self.ship_cards without
-- re-deriving ship/faction lookups.
-- Builds every cell at its UNSCROLLED base position (scroll ignored here
-- entirely) and records that base position on each card (`base_cy`/
-- `base_strip_cy`/`cell_x`) - apply_forsale_scroll_display (below) is what
-- actually moves cells to the current scroll position afterwards, every
-- time it changes, without needing to rebuild/reparent any nodes. Kept
-- separate from that positioning step so a data-driven rebuild (ship
-- bought/sold, tab reopened) doesn't have to know or care what the current
-- scroll/animation state is - it always regenerates the same base layout,
-- and whoever calls this also calls apply_forsale_scroll_display right
-- after to place it correctly (see refresh_ship_cards).
local function build_ship_forsale_grid(out_list, panel_node, rows)
	local panel_pos = gui.get_position(panel_node)
	local panel_size = gui.get_size(panel_node)
	local left = panel_pos.x - panel_size.x * 0.5 + GRID_GUTTER
	local top = panel_pos.y + panel_size.y * 0.5 - GRID_GUTTER

	if #rows == 0 then
		local pos = vmath.vector3(panel_pos.x, top - GRID_CELL_H * 0.5, 0)
		local label = gui.new_text_node(pos, "(none)")
		gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(label, vmath.vector4(0.5, 0.55, 0.6, 1.0))
		gui.set_parent(label, panel_node, true)
		table.insert(out_list, { box = nil, label = label, item_key = nil, source = "ship_forsale" })
		return
	end

	for _, row in ipairs(rows) do
		local class_key, role_key = row.item_key:match("^(%a+)_(%a+)$")
		local col = (ROLE_COL[role_key] or 1) - 1
		local grid_row = (CLASS_ROW[class_key] or 1) - 1
		local cx = left + col * (GRID_CELL_W + GRID_GUTTER) + GRID_CELL_W * 0.5
		local base_cy = top - grid_row * (GRID_CELL_H + GRID_GUTTER) - GRID_CELL_H * 0.5

		local box = gui.new_box_node(vmath.vector3(cx, base_cy, 0), vmath.vector3(GRID_CELL_W, GRID_CELL_H, 0))
		gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(box, row.color or CARD_COLOR)
		gui.set_parent(box, panel_node, true)

		-- Name+price strip along the bottom edge of the cell. The render
		-- script's live 3D overlay is the LAST thing drawn each frame, after
		-- the normal GUI pass (render/custom.render_script's own header
		-- comment) - so it would paint OVER this strip if given the cell's
		-- full rect. compute_forsale_preview_items (below, near
		-- show_ship_detail) instead sends only the rect ABOVE this strip
		-- (cell height minus SHIP_LABEL_H) as the 3D viewport, so the strip
		-- always stays on top, never buried under the model.
		local base_strip_cy = base_cy - GRID_CELL_H * 0.5 + SHIP_LABEL_H * 0.5
		local strip = gui.new_box_node(vmath.vector3(cx, base_strip_cy, 0), vmath.vector3(GRID_CELL_W, SHIP_LABEL_H, 0))
		gui.set_pivot(strip, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(strip, vmath.vector4(0.03, 0.035, 0.05, 0.85))
		gui.set_parent(strip, panel_node, true)

		local label = gui.new_text_node(vmath.vector3(cx, base_strip_cy + SHIP_NAME_OFFSET_Y, 0), row.label)
		gui.set_pivot(label, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(label, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(label, vmath.vector4(0.9, 0.95, 1.0, 1.0))
		gui.set_scale(label, SHIP_NAME_SCALE)
		gui.set_parent(label, panel_node, true)

		local detail = gui.new_text_node(vmath.vector3(cx, base_strip_cy + SHIP_DETAIL_OFFSET_Y, 0), row.detail or "")
		gui.set_pivot(detail, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_font(detail, "default_font")
		gui.set_color(detail, vmath.vector4(0.7, 0.76, 0.84, 1.0))
		gui.set_scale(detail, SHIP_DETAIL_SCALE)
		gui.set_parent(detail, panel_node, true)

		table.insert(out_list, {
			box = box, label = label, detail = detail, strip = strip,
			item_key = row.item_key, source = "ship_forsale",
			model_path = row.model_path,
			cell_x = cx, base_cy = base_cy, base_strip_cy = base_strip_cy,
		})
	end
end

-- Moves every Ships-For-Sale card to `self.scroll_display.forsale` (the
-- CURRENTLY DISPLAYED scroll position, which may still be animating toward
-- the real target `self.scroll.forsale` - see SCROLL_LERP_SPEED's own
-- comment) by offsetting each card's own recorded base position, rather
-- than rebuilding the card list. Called right after build_ship_forsale_grid
-- (to place the freshly-built base layout at the current display
-- position), and every frame update() is actively animating toward a new
-- target, and immediately (no lerp) while actively drag-scrolling.
local function apply_forsale_scroll_display(self)
	local offset = self.scroll_display.forsale
	-- card.box/label/strip are reparented under forsale_panel_node (purely
	-- for its stencil clipping, see init()), so gui.set_position on them
	-- takes a position relative to that panel, NOT the absolute screen
	-- coordinates card.cell_x/base_cy/base_strip_cy were computed in
	-- (build_ship_forsale_grid records those before the reparent happens).
	-- Subtracting the panel's own position here converts back to that
	-- relative space - omitting this double-applies the panel's offset and
	-- flings every cell (and its click hit-target) off-screen, since a
	-- prior version of this function set the absolute coordinates directly.
	local panel_pos = gui.get_position(self.forsale_panel_node)
	for _, card in ipairs(self.ship_cards) do
		if card.source == "ship_forsale" and card.box and card.base_cy then
			gui.set_position(card.box, vmath.vector3(card.cell_x - panel_pos.x, card.base_cy + offset - panel_pos.y, 0))
			local strip_y = card.base_strip_cy + offset - panel_pos.y
			gui.set_position(card.label, vmath.vector3(card.cell_x - panel_pos.x, strip_y + SHIP_NAME_OFFSET_Y, 0))
			if card.detail then
				gui.set_position(card.detail, vmath.vector3(card.cell_x - panel_pos.x, strip_y + SHIP_DETAIL_OFFSET_Y, 0))
			end
			if card.strip then
				gui.set_position(card.strip, vmath.vector3(card.cell_x - panel_pos.x, strip_y, 0))
			end
		end
	end
end

-- Shows/hides and sizes the Ships-For-Sale scrollbar (track + thumb) and
-- up/down chevrons - all four are static nodes authored in main/outpost.gui
-- (panel-level chrome, not per-card), just toggled/repositioned here.
-- Nothing to scroll at all (every for-sale ship fits on screen at once)
-- hides all four rather than showing a scrollbar that can't move.
local function refresh_forsale_scrollbar(self)
	local max_scroll = max_scroll_for_grid()
	local scrollable = max_scroll > 0
	gui.set_enabled(self.forsale_scrollbar_track, scrollable)
	gui.set_enabled(self.forsale_scrollbar_thumb, scrollable)
	gui.set_enabled(self.forsale_scroll_up, scrollable and self.scroll_display.forsale > 0.5)
	gui.set_enabled(self.forsale_scroll_down, scrollable and self.scroll_display.forsale < max_scroll - 0.5)
	if not scrollable then
		return
	end
	local grid_rows = #CLASS_ORDER
	local content_height = (grid_rows - 1) * (GRID_CELL_H + GRID_GUTTER) + GRID_CELL_H
	local track_pos = gui.get_position(self.forsale_scrollbar_track)
	local track_size = gui.get_size(self.forsale_scrollbar_track)
	local visible_fraction = math.min(1, SHIPS_PANEL_HEIGHT / content_height)
	local thumb_h = math.max(20, track_size.y * visible_fraction)
	local scroll_fraction = self.scroll_display.forsale / max_scroll
	local thumb_travel = track_size.y - thumb_h
	local thumb_y = track_pos.y + track_size.y / 2 - thumb_h / 2 - scroll_fraction * thumb_travel
	gui.set_size(self.forsale_scrollbar_thumb, vmath.vector3(track_size.x, thumb_h, 0))
	gui.set_position(self.forsale_scrollbar_thumb, vmath.vector3(track_pos.x, thumb_y, 0))
end

local function refresh_ship_cards(self)
	clear_card_list(self.ship_cards)
	self.ship_cards = {}
	local faction = session.get_faction()
	local active_ship = session.get_ship_id()
	local forsale = ship_forsale_rows(faction, active_ship)
	self.scroll.forsale = math.min(self.scroll.forsale, max_scroll_for_grid())
	-- A data-driven rebuild (ship bought/sold, tab reopened) snaps straight
	-- to the target - no animation here, only actual user-scroll-input
	-- (wheel/drag) eases via update()'s own per-frame lerp.
	self.scroll_display.forsale = self.scroll.forsale
	build_ship_forsale_grid(self.ship_cards, self.forsale_panel_node, forsale)
	apply_forsale_scroll_display(self)
	refresh_forsale_preview(self)
	refresh_forsale_scrollbar(self)
end

-- Recolors/resizes every one of init()'s 58 map dots and updates
-- "map_current_text" - called on entering the Map tab, not at init() time,
-- since session.get_faction() isn't decided yet when outpost.gui_script's
-- own init() runs (main.collection embeds start_screen/faction_select/
-- outpost together, §3's project-layout note - faction is only chosen
-- once the player clicks through faction_select). The "current" system is
-- the one whose outpost the player is docked at (docked_system_id).
local function refresh_map(self)
	local faction = session.get_faction()
	local current_id = docked_system_id()
	local current_name = (current_id and star_systems.SYSTEMS[current_id] and star_systems.SYSTEMS[current_id].name) or "-"
	gui.set_text(self.map_current_text_node, "Current system: " .. current_name)
	for id, dot in pairs(self.map_dots) do
		local is_current = id == current_id
		local color = is_current and MAP_CURRENT_COLOR or MAP_DOT_COLOR
		local size = is_current and MAP_DOT_SIZE_CURRENT or MAP_DOT_SIZE
		gui.set_color(dot.box, color)
		gui.set_color(dot.label, color)
		gui.set_size(dot.box, size)
	end
end

-- Loads main/images/skins/<name>.jpg as a GUI texture once per Skins-tab
-- visit (same sys.load_resource + gui.new_texture route show_ship_silhouette
-- uses for the top-down images). Returns the texture id, or nil if missing.
local function skin_texture(self, name)
	if self.skin_textures[name] then
		return self.skin_textures[name]
	end
	local data = sys.load_resource("/main/images/skins/" .. name .. ".jpg")
	local img = data and image.load(data)
	if not img then
		print("[outpost] no skin thumbnail for " .. name)
		return nil
	end
	local texture_id = "skin_" .. name
	if not gui.new_texture(texture_id, img.width, img.height, img.type, img.buffer, false) then
		return nil
	end
	self.skin_textures[name] = texture_id
	return texture_id
end

-- Deletes the Skins tab's cards (each card's nodes are children of its box)
-- and, when leaving the tab, the thumbnail textures they used.
local function clear_skin_cards(self, free_textures)
	for _, card in ipairs(self.skin_cards) do
		gui.delete_node(card.box)
	end
	self.skin_cards = {}
	if free_textures then
		for _, texture_id in pairs(self.skin_textures) do
			gui.delete_texture(texture_id)
		end
		self.skin_textures = {}
	end
end

-- One row per skin for the active ship in the player's faction, cheapest
-- first, with the default hull (skin_id nil) always first.
local function skin_rows()
	local ship_id = session.get_ship_id()
	local faction = session.get_faction()
	local equipped = session.get_equipped_skin(ship_id)
	local species
	local rows = {}
	for skin_id, skin in pairs(skins.SKINS) do
		if skin.chassis == ship_id and skin.faction == faction then
			species = skin.ship
			local collection = skin_collections.COLLECTIONS[skin.collection]
			table.insert(rows, {
				skin_id = skin_id,
				name = collection and collection.name or skin.collection,
				thumb = skin.ship .. "-" .. skin.collection,
				price = session.get_skin_price(skin_id) or 0,
				owned = session.is_skin_owned(skin_id),
				equipped = equipped == skin_id,
			})
		end
	end
	table.sort(rows, function(a, b)
		if a.price ~= b.price then
			return a.price < b.price
		end
		return a.name < b.name
	end)
	if species then
		table.insert(rows, 1, { skin_id = nil, name = "Default", thumb = species .. "-default", owned = true, equipped = equipped == nil })
	end
	return rows
end

local function new_skin_text(pos, text, scale, parent)
	local node = gui.new_text_node(pos, text)
	gui.set_pivot(node, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
	gui.set_font(node, "default_font")
	gui.set_color(node, vmath.vector4(0.9, 0.95, 1.0, 1.0))
	gui.set_scale(node, vmath.vector3(scale, scale, 1))
	gui.set_parent(node, parent, true)
	return node
end

local function refresh_skin_cards(self)
	clear_skin_cards(self, false)
	gui.set_text(self.skins_heading_node, "Skins  --  " .. ship_display_name(session.get_ship_id(), session.get_faction()))

	local panel = self.skins_panel_node
	local panel_pos = gui.get_position(panel)
	local panel_size = gui.get_size(panel)
	local left = panel_pos.x - panel_size.x * 0.5 + GRID_GUTTER
	local top = panel_pos.y + panel_size.y * 0.5 - GRID_GUTTER

	local rows = skin_rows()
	if #rows == 0 then
		local label = new_skin_text(vmath.vector3(panel_pos.x, top - SKIN_CELL_H * 0.5, 0), "(no skins for this ship yet)", 1, panel)
		table.insert(self.skin_cards, { box = label })
		return
	end

	for i, row in ipairs(rows) do
		local col = (i - 1) % SKIN_GRID_COLS
		local grid_row = math.floor((i - 1) / SKIN_GRID_COLS)
		local cx = left + col * (SKIN_CELL_W + GRID_GUTTER) + SKIN_CELL_W * 0.5
		local cy = top - grid_row * (SKIN_CELL_H + GRID_GUTTER) - SKIN_CELL_H * 0.5

		-- Frame: the box itself, its color showing as a border around the
		-- thumbnail - green equipped, blue owned, plain card color otherwise.
		local box = gui.new_box_node(vmath.vector3(cx, cy, 0), vmath.vector3(SKIN_CELL_W, SKIN_CELL_H, 0))
		gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(box, row.equipped and SKIN_EQUIPPED_COLOR or (row.owned and SKIN_OWNED_COLOR) or CARD_COLOR)
		gui.set_parent(box, panel, true)

		-- Thumbnail area: inside the frame, above the name strip.
		local area_w = SKIN_CELL_W - 2 * SKIN_FRAME
		local area_h = SKIN_CELL_H - 2 * SKIN_FRAME - GRID_LABEL_H
		local area_pos = vmath.vector3(cx, cy + GRID_LABEL_H * 0.5, 0)
		local bg = gui.new_box_node(area_pos, vmath.vector3(area_w, area_h, 0))
		gui.set_pivot(bg, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(bg, row.owned and SKIN_THUMB_BG or vmath.mul_per_elem(SKIN_THUMB_BG, SKIN_LOCKED_TINT))
		gui.set_parent(bg, box, true)

		local texture_id = skin_texture(self, row.thumb)
		if texture_id then
			local thumb_h = math.min(area_h, area_w / SKIN_THUMB_ASPECT)
			local thumb = gui.new_box_node(area_pos, vmath.vector3(thumb_h * SKIN_THUMB_ASPECT, thumb_h, 0))
			gui.set_pivot(thumb, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_size_mode(thumb, gui.SIZE_MODE_MANUAL)
			gui.set_texture(thumb, texture_id)
			gui.set_color(thumb, row.owned and vmath.vector4(1, 1, 1, 1) or SKIN_LOCKED_TINT)
			gui.set_parent(thumb, box, true)
		end

		local strip_pos = vmath.vector3(cx, cy - SKIN_CELL_H * 0.5 + SKIN_FRAME + GRID_LABEL_H * 0.5, 0)
		local strip = gui.new_box_node(strip_pos, vmath.vector3(area_w, GRID_LABEL_H, 0))
		gui.set_pivot(strip, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
		gui.set_color(strip, vmath.vector4(0.03, 0.035, 0.05, 0.9))
		gui.set_parent(strip, box, true)
		new_skin_text(strip_pos, row.name, 0.8, box)

		-- Status badge, top-left of the thumbnail: EQUIPPED / OWNED / price.
		-- The default hull only gets one while equipped.
		local badge_text, badge_color
		if row.equipped then
			badge_text, badge_color = "EQUIPPED", vmath.mul_per_elem(SKIN_EQUIPPED_COLOR, SKIN_BADGE_SHADE)
		elseif row.skin_id and row.owned then
			badge_text, badge_color = "OWNED", vmath.mul_per_elem(SKIN_OWNED_COLOR, SKIN_BADGE_SHADE)
		elseif row.skin_id then
			badge_text, badge_color = row.price .. " Scrip", SKIN_PRICE_BADGE_COLOR
		end
		if badge_text then
			local badge_pos = vmath.vector3(cx - SKIN_CELL_W * 0.5 + SKIN_FRAME + SKIN_BADGE_SIZE.x * 0.5,
				cy + SKIN_CELL_H * 0.5 - SKIN_FRAME - SKIN_BADGE_SIZE.y * 0.5, 0)
			local badge = gui.new_box_node(badge_pos, SKIN_BADGE_SIZE)
			gui.set_pivot(badge, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
			gui.set_color(badge, badge_color)
			gui.set_parent(badge, box, true)
			new_skin_text(badge_pos, badge_text, 0.7, box)
		end

		table.insert(self.skin_cards, {
			box = box, skin_id = row.skin_id, owned = row.owned, equipped = row.equipped,
		})
	end
end

-- Switches between the Overview, Fitting, and Ships tabs: toggles which
-- static panels are visible and (re)builds whichever tab's dynamic
-- content is now showing. Dynamic content for a tab being LEFT is
-- cleared rather than just hidden, same "delete + recreate on next
-- visit" pattern already used elsewhere on this screen - Overview has no
-- per-visit dynamic nodes of its own, just its text refreshed on entry.
local function set_page(self, page)
	self.active_page = page
	hide_weapon_arc(self) -- don't leave the weapon firing-arc hover wedge stuck on-screen after leaving Fitting
	gui.set_color(self.tab_nodes.overview, page == "overview" and TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE or TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE)
	gui.set_color(self.tab_nodes.fitting, page == "fitting" and TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE or TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE)
	gui.set_color(self.tab_nodes.ships, page == "ships" and TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE or TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE)
	gui.set_color(self.tab_nodes.map, page == "map" and TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE or TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE)
	gui.set_color(self.tab_nodes.skins, page == "skins" and TAB_COLOR_ACTIVE or TAB_COLOR_INACTIVE)
	for _, node in ipairs(self.skins_static_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, page == "skins")
	end
	if page ~= "skins" then
		clear_skin_cards(self, true)
	end
	for _, node in ipairs(self.overview_static_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, page == "overview")
	end
	for _, node in ipairs(self.fitting_static_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, page == "fitting")
	end
	for _, node in ipairs(self.ships_static_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, page == "ships")
	end
	for _, node in ipairs(self.map_static_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, page == "map")
	end
	for _, dot in pairs(self.map_dots) do
		gui.set_enabled(dot.box, page == "map")
		gui.set_enabled(dot.label, page == "map")
	end

	if page == "overview" then
		clear_hover(self)
		clear_markers(self)
		clear_card_list(self.cards)
		self.cards = {}
		clear_card_list(self.ship_cards)
		self.ship_cards = {}
		refresh_overview(self)
	elseif page == "fitting" then
		clear_card_list(self.ship_cards)
		self.ship_cards = {}
		build_markers(self)
		refresh_markers(self)
		refresh_filter_buttons(self)
		refresh_module_cards(self)
	elseif page == "ships" then
		clear_hover(self)
		clear_markers(self)
		clear_card_list(self.cards)
		self.cards = {}
		refresh_ship_cards(self)
	elseif page == "skins" then
		clear_hover(self)
		clear_markers(self)
		clear_card_list(self.cards)
		self.cards = {}
		clear_card_list(self.ship_cards)
		self.ship_cards = {}
		refresh_skin_cards(self)
	else -- "map"
		clear_hover(self)
		clear_markers(self)
		clear_card_list(self.cards)
		self.cards = {}
		clear_card_list(self.ship_cards)
		self.ship_cards = {}
		refresh_map(self)
	end

	-- Leaving the Ships tab entirely (overview/fitting): clears the
	-- Ships-For-Sale grid's live previews, same as refresh_ship_cards
	-- already does when the tab stays open but its content changes -
	-- harmless/idempotent to call again for the "ships" branch above, which
	-- already sent this via refresh_ship_cards.
	refresh_forsale_preview(self)
end

local function hits(node, action)
	return node and gui.pick_node(node, action.x, action.y)
end

-- gui.pick_node is a plain bounding-box test - it does NOT know about a
-- panel's own stencil clipping, so a card scrolled/spilled outside its
-- panel's visible bounds would otherwise still register as clickable at
-- its (invisible) raw position. Card lists are only ever scrolled as a
-- whole panel, so every card's owning panel is fully determined by its
-- `source` - used as an extra gate (alongside hits(card.box, action)) so
-- a click only counts if it also lands inside that panel's own bounds
-- (plan.md §4).
local function panel_for_card(self, card)
	if card.source == "shop" then return self.shop_panel_node end
	if card.source == "owned" then return self.owned_panel_node end
	if card.source == "ship_forsale" then return self.forsale_panel_node end
	return nil
end

-- `ref` is either a catalog item_key (source == "shop": buying always
-- grants a brand-new INSTANCE, which doesn't exist until the item is
-- actually dropped on a slot, §4) or an owned instance id (source ==
-- "slot"/"owned": a SPECIFIC physical copy, possibly upgraded, is being
-- moved).
local function start_drag(self, source, ref, from_slot, action)
	local item_key, instance_id, inst
	if source == "shop" then
		item_key = ref
	else
		instance_id = ref
		inst = session.get_instance(ref)
		item_key = inst and inst.item_key
	end
	local module = catalog.get(item_key)
	self.dragging = {
		source = source, item_key = item_key, instance_id = instance_id,
		from_slot = from_slot, type = module and module.type,
	}
	local pos = vmath.vector3(action.x, action.y, 0)
	local box = gui.new_box_node(pos, GHOST_SIZE)
	gui.set_pivot(box, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
	gui.set_color(box, vmath.vector4(0.95, 0.85, 0.3, 0.85))
	-- Icon, not the item's name as text (direct instruction) - same
	-- ICON_TEXTURE atlas and per-type SLOT_EMPTY_ICON fallback every other
	-- icon in this file already uses, so a module with no catalog icon of
	-- its own still shows something meaningful while being dragged.
	local icon = gui.new_box_node(pos, GHOST_ICON_SIZE)
	gui.set_pivot(icon, gui.PIVOT_CENTER)
	gui.set_texture(icon, ICON_TEXTURE)
	gui.set_size_mode(icon, gui.SIZE_MODE_MANUAL)
	local icon_id = (module and module.icon) or (module and SLOT_EMPTY_ICON[module.type])
	if icon_id then
		gui.play_flipbook(icon, icon_id)
	end
	self.ghost = { box = box, icon = icon }
end

-- Opens the confirmation dialog with `message`, deferring the actual
-- effect until the player clicks Confirm - `on_confirm` runs then (and
-- only then); clicking Cancel just closes the dialog and does nothing.
-- Per direct instruction: any purchase or swap goes through this rather
-- than taking effect immediately on drop/click.
--
-- `show_cancel` (optional, defaults to true) - direct instruction: a
-- purely informational notice with nothing to actually confirm (e.g. "this
-- slot needs the ship advanced first") should show only the one
-- acknowledge button, not a Confirm/Cancel PAIR implying there's a choice
-- to make. When false, Yes is re-centered on the panel (rather than left
-- of a hidden Cancel, leaving an empty gap) and Cancel/No stays disabled.
local function show_confirm(self, message, on_confirm, show_cancel)
	if show_cancel == nil then
		show_cancel = true
	end
	self.confirm = { on_confirm = on_confirm }
	gui.set_text(self.confirm_nodes.message, message)
	for _, node in pairs(self.confirm_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, true)
	end
	gui.set_enabled(self.confirm_nodes.no, show_cancel)
	gui.set_enabled(self.confirm_nodes.no_label, show_cancel)
	local yes_pos = vmath.vector3(show_cancel and CONFIRM_YES_X or CONFIRM_YES_CENTERED_X, CONFIRM_BUTTON_Y, 0)
	gui.set_position(self.confirm_nodes.yes, yes_pos)
	gui.set_position(self.confirm_nodes.yes_label, yes_pos)
	refresh_forsale_preview(self) -- hide the Ships grid's 3D previews under the dialog
end

local function hide_confirm(self)
	self.confirm = nil
	for _, node in pairs(self.confirm_nodes) do
		gui.set_enabled(node, false)
	end
	refresh_forsale_preview(self) -- bring the Ships grid's previews back
end

-- Ship detail modal preview, keyed by model path (§2.8.9) - PREVIEW_WORLD_POS
-- is a THIRD-ATTEMPT live 3D overlay. Attempts 1 and 2 (a render-target/
-- offscreen-camera pipeline referenced either only from the render script,
-- or also from a GUI scene's own `resources{}` block) were both rolled
-- back - attempt 1 rendered nothing but a white square; attempt 2 froze the
-- entire browser tab solid the moment the modal opened, a much more
-- serious failure. This attempt uses neither mechanism:
-- `render/custom.render_script` draws the `model` predicate a second time,
-- in a small on-screen viewport that overlaps `ship_detail_preview`
-- exactly, as the LAST thing each frame - aimed at whichever off-world
-- position this table gives for the ship's model. Toggled/aimed purely by
-- messaging the render script itself (`msg.post("@render:",
-- "set_ship_preview", ...)`, the same "@render:" pathway the stock default
-- render script's own comments document) - never a GUI resource
-- declaration, never a message to a game-object component.
--
-- Originally paired with a PREVIEW_IMAGES static top-down PNG as a
-- guaranteed-visible base layer underneath (the live 3D pass draws over it
-- when active) - removed per direct instruction once every ship actually
-- had a live 3D preview of its own, so there was nothing left for it to be
-- a fallback FOR. `ship_detail_preview` is a plain GUI box node with no
-- texture/flipbook now - if this table is ever rolled back, static images
-- would need reintroducing alongside it (see git history for
-- PREVIEW_IMAGES's exact old contents/wiring).
--
-- A ship with no entry here has no preview art at all yet - honest
-- placeholder (plain background box) instead of faking one.
local PREVIEW_WORLD_POS = {
	["/assets/models/patrol_interceptor/patrol_interceptor.model"] = vmath.vector3(100000, 0, 0),
	["/assets/models/escort_interceptor/escort_interceptor.model"] = vmath.vector3(100000, 1000, 0),
