-- Per-model camera distance/far-plane, sent alongside PREVIEW_WORLD_POS's
-- target position. A single fixed eye offset (the render script's own
-- PREVIEW_EYE_OFFSET_DEFAULT) was tuned against the hand-authored escort_interceptor
-- hull's ~6.3-unit world-space bounding radius; real user-supplied assets
-- range up to ~20x that (computed via a full scene-graph traversal - node
-- transforms compose non-trivially, e.g. sardine.glb's own 100x/0.01x
-- parent-node scale pair - not just eyeballing a raw accessor min/max).
-- Left at that fixed distance, barracuda/falcon rendered as an
-- unrecognizable extreme close-up, and the far plane clipped the bigger
-- ones outright. Each entry here is (0, 3, 8) scaled uniformly by (this
-- model's own radius / 6.34) - a bounding-sphere-tight fit - and THEN by a
-- further flat 1.5x zoom-out on top (per direct instruction: the tight fit
-- looked cropped/claustrophobic even when technically nothing was clipped,
-- especially once the static fallback image was removed and the 3D view
-- became the only thing on screen). `far` is (distance + radius + 50)
-- margin, computed against the POST-zoom-out distance so the bigger models
-- still don't clip. Keyed by model_path (the .model file), same key
-- PREVIEW_WORLD_POS uses - NOT by the mesh's own .glb/.gltf filename. A
-- ship with no entry here just uses the render script's own built-in
-- (also zoomed-out) defaults.
local PREVIEW_CAMERA = {
	-- Generated by tools/integrate_fleet_models.py from actual GLB bounds.
	-- Square 45-degree preview checked over a full rotation; sale cards are wider.
	["/assets/models/patrol_interceptor/patrol_interceptor.model"] = { eye_offset = vmath.vector3(0.000, 9.200, 24.600), far = 90.000 },
